00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:39.610
March the 15th, 1768.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.520
"I finished a tragedy called
'The Mysterious Mother,'

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:45.744
which I'd begun December the 25th, 1768,

00:11:45.744 --> 00:11:48.090
but I'd later decide for several months

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:49.470
while I went to Paris,

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.420
and while I was writing my 'Historic Doubts

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:53.367
on the reign of Richard III'

00:11:54.242 --> 00:11:55.310
the last two acts were not now

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:58.720
as much finished as I intended."

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:00.170
Thus wrote Horace Walpole,

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:02.910
in "Short Notes of the
Life of Horatio Walpole,"

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:04.810
a draft autobiographical account

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:06.460
of his life, his letters,

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:08.680
and his most formative
experiences to date,

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.490
that he drew up in 1779.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.160
Now it's often forgotten,

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.700
that "The Mysterious Mother"

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:19.740
was not Walpole's first foray into drama

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:22.030
and nor would it prove to be his last.

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.360
As early as June 1743,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.020
he published in the journal "Old England,"

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:28.450
"The Dear Witches,"

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:31.130
a short dramatic reworking of "MacBeth,"

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.870
that figured his father Sir Robert Walpole

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.020
as the Duncan-like victim to a cabal

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:38.713
of political assassinators,

00:12:40.044 --> 00:12:42.820
and later in 1773, he
wrote a short comedy.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.350
"Nature Will Prevail,"

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.480
which was performed at the little theater,

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:49.510
in the Hay Market in June 1775,

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:50.890
and he published this together

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.330
with two very interesting essays,

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:55.190
entitled "Thoughts on Tragedy"

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:56.410
and "Thoughts on Comedy,"

00:12:56.410 --> 00:13:00.280
in volume two of his
posthumous works in 1798.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:02.550
We remember the "Thoughts on Tragedy"

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.670
had initially been formulated in letters

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:08.450
to the dramatist Robert Jephson in 1775.

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:11.210
The same playwright
who would later produce

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:13.470
a dramatic hybrid of both

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:16.190
"The Castle of Otranto"
and "The Mysterious Mother"

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.889
in "The Count of Narbonne" in 1781.

00:13:19.889 --> 00:13:23.820
Completed by the middle of March 1768,

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:25.680
"The Mysterious Mother" was immediately

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:28.150
dispatched in manuscript form,

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.390
to some of Walpole's closest friends,

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.320
and within a month,

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.090
it had gratifyingly received

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.930
the approbation of his oracle of taste.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.440
Of course John Chute,

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.810
who is not easily pleased,

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:43.280
and Thomas Gray who is
still more difficult.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:46.820
"As you would not bear the
subject," he continued,

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:48.510
in a letter to George Montague,

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.010
in April of that year,

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:52.110
"I will say no more of it,"

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.472
before somewhat curiously claiming,

00:13:54.472 --> 00:13:58.720
"that I'm not yet
intoxicated enough with it,

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.290
to think it would do for the stage,

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.353
though I wish to see it acted."

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:07.090
Walpole's expression here of ambivalent,

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:10.530
if not altogether paradoxical sentiments,

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:13.540
that his desire to see the play acted,

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:16.360
without it being performed on stage,

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.010
is central, I would argue,

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:19.600
to "The Mysterious Mother,"

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.130
and sentiments that are further evidenced

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:25.290
in his making of really
quite specific plans

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.320
for the involvement of his friends,

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.340
in the reading, performance,

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:31.590
and visual representation of the play.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:34.260
Among them of course, Katherine Clive.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.910
Hannah Pritchard. William
Mason, and Diana Beauclerk,

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:41.809
even as he continuously
decries its horrid,

00:14:41.809 --> 00:14:46.343
disgusting, and ultimately
unperformable subject matter.

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:50.310
So, upon learning the year
after its completion,

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.972
that friends of Montagu,

00:14:52.972 --> 00:14:55.900
had eagerly consumed the copy of the play

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.630
that Walpole had sent him.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:01.550
Walpole, citing a line
from "Henry the IV." part I,

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:05.290
wrote that "I beg you would
keep it under lock and key;

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:08.630
it is not at all food for the public--

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:11.670
at least not till I am food for worms,

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.320
good Percy."

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:16.960
Now, I think this thus begins a complex process

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:19.670
of disclosure and obfuscation,

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:22.210
publication and suppression,

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:24.580
the assertion and the concealment

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:25.810
of authority and identity,

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:28.200
that characterizes the textual life

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:32.710
of the play from the moments
of its completion onwards.

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:34.210
Between the 14th of June,

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:37.433
and the 6th of August
of that year, 1768,

00:15:37.433 --> 00:15:42.330
Walpole prints 50 copies of
the script at Strawberry Hill.

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:46.580
This in itself I think is a
significant publishing choice.

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:50.030
Unlike "Otranto," which in order to maintain

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:53.900
the antiquarian conceit
of the discovered document

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:56.290
had been published by
Thomas Lanz in London,

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:58.250
some three and a half years before.

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:00.500
"The Mysterious Mother"
was literary property

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.590
that required tight
regulation and control.

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:06.662
Printed versions, consequently,

00:16:06.662 --> 00:16:10.890
were distributed to only a select few

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.200
of Walpole's most trusted friends,

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:17.280
among them Thomas Barret, Richard
Bull and Diana Beauclerk.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.890
Now Walpole's manuscript annotations

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:22.070
in the copy that he sends to Bull,

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.330
indicate the extent to which

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:27.830
he policed the circulation
of the printed text.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:29.320
This I what he writes.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.590
"Mr. Walpole has sent Mr. Bull

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.020
the only copy he has left on the play,

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.200
but begs to have it returned,

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:37.500
as soon as he has read it,

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:40.810
and that he will not let it
go out of his hands."

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.696
But later in June 1779,

00:16:43.696 --> 00:16:45.730
when Walpole eventually allows

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:48.130
Horace Mann to see a copy of the play

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:50.900
that he had long discussed
in letters to him,

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.524
he similarly asked that
it be returned to him

00:16:53.524 --> 00:16:55.910
via post all the way from Florence.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:59.950
Walpole's reluctance to
part with printed scripts,

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:03.690
encouraged people to
make of it manuscript copies

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:07.110
and at least seven of these
manuscript, fair copies

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:09.490
are held at The Lewis
Walpole Library today.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:13.020
However, so as to
forestall the publication

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:16.300
of a spurious and unauthorized
edition of the play,

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:18.830
the upsetting news of
which he had received

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:20.800
from the actor John Henderson,

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.700
Walpole reluctantly gave his consent

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.310
to J. Dodsley in London to publish

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:29.660
"The Mysterious Mother" in 1781.

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:33.840
As he wrote in a suitably
gothic idiom to Cole,

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:36.300
"my tragedy has wandered into the hands

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:38.620
of some banditti booksellers,

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:40.870
and I am forced to publish it myself

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:42.740
to prevent piracy.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:47.740
All I can do is condemn it
myself, and that I shall."

00:17:47.795 --> 00:17:51.950
Unlike the first Strawberry
Hill edition of 1768,

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.500
this second edition bore no mention

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:56.580
of Walpole on its title page,

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:00.530
but included instead a one
and a half page preface,

00:18:00.530 --> 00:18:03.269
that stressed the undisclosed author's

00:18:03.269 --> 00:18:07.880
partly sincere, partly
faux modest reluctance

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:09.320
to bring it to public view.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.430
You can see this on the slide.

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:13.530
"All the favor the author solicits

00:18:14.537 --> 00:18:15.370
or expects," he writes,

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.660
"is to be believed how unwillingly

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:19.880
he has submitted to its appearance.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:22.720
He cannot be more blamed,

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.010
than he blames himself

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:25.190
for having undertaken,

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.140
so disagreeable a story,

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:29.560
for having hazarded the publicity,

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:31.850
by letting it get out of his own hands."

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:36.330
There's a lot of talk about
hands, around this play.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:38.170
Happily the mere advertising,

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:40.627
of this forthcoming authorized edition,

00:18:40.627 --> 00:18:44.050
was enough to forestall
the intended piracy.

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.670
What happens is that the 1781 text,

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:47.850
though it's printed,

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.680
never actually proceeds to publication,

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:52.210
and Walpole is now content,

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.940
to give copies of this 1781 edition

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:56.090
to some of his friends,

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:59.763
in lieu of the first edition of 1768.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:03.600
Having read this 1781 edition,

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:07.110
and urging its immediate
theatrical production,

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:10.280
David Erskine Baker in 1782,

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:13.090
writes that "knowing its merit,

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:16.650
we cannot but express our surprise,

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:20.260
that its author should wish to
withhold it from the public."

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:22.180
But I think by 1785,

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:23.860
Walpole is very clearly aware

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:26.090
of the fact that the play had become

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:30.030
somewhat of a collector's
object, or a rarity.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.880
In fact, I'd go so far as to say,

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.796
he actively courted the sense of intrigue

00:19:34.796 --> 00:19:36.930
that this entailed.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:41.040
But the text publication
history does not end here.

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:44.940
November 1781, extracts of the play,

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:48.340
that had purportedly been
leaked from a manuscript copy,

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:50.880
were published in the
"St. James's Chronicle."

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:54.810
Now the newspapers, reprinting of Act I,

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.200
scene one and five,

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.210
was preceded by lines
that demonstrate that

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:02.820
"The Mysterious Mother"
had come to be regarded

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.968
as highly desirable literary property,

00:20:05.968 --> 00:20:10.080
made all the more so by
the mystery, the secrecy,

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:13.490
the exclusivity, that
surrounded its circulation.

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:16.620
This is what the reprinting
of those two scenes

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:19.290
from Act I is preceded by.

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:22.020
"An anonymous correspondent
who highly favored

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:23.550
the "St. James's Chronicle,"

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:25.610
by the following desirable specimen

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:27.430
of an unpublished performance

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:28.660
which in the dramatic world,

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.260
we are truly sensible,

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.880
is held as a first rate curiosity."

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.590
I'm interested in that word curiosity,

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:36.860
as a kind of antiquarian object.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:40.580
But the same paper followed
up this piracy one week later.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:42.610
This time publishing Act III.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:44.293
Scenes one and three, and Act IV scene IV,

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:48.680
in the regular poet's corner column.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.580
Prefacing the extracts
with a similar affirmation

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:53.157
of the play's status as a rare

00:20:53.157 --> 00:20:56.180
yet highly desirable artifact.

00:20:56.180 --> 00:20:58.645
To the printer of the
"St. James's Chronicle,"

00:20:58.645 --> 00:21:01.510
your readiness to oblige,

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:04.990
induces me to transcribe
for your poet's corner

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:08.920
another speech or two from
an invaluable manuscript.

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:11.370
Whilst it remains in my possession."

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:13.210
Further extracts are printed

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:16.100
in the "Public Advertiser" in November 1781.

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:18.590
Again in 1783,

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.340
but it's this latter 1783 leakage

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:22.800
of aspects of the text,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.640
that prompts Walpole to
write to the publisher,

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:27.450
Henry Samson Woodfall,

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:29.890
in request of its immediate suppression,

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.400
even offering him financial compensation

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.380
for any imaginary benefit he says,

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:38.070
that he might receive from the impression.

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:40.580
At this time writes Walpole,

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:43.110
"the public must be curious to see,

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.510
more interesting articles,

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:49.370
than scenes of an old tragedy
on a disgusting subject,

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:52.810
a play which the author
thinks so little worthy

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:55.850
of being published that after
the first small impression

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:57.700
he is endeavored to suppress it,

00:21:57.700 --> 00:21:59.643
as much as lies in his power."

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:04.510
1790, Walpole learned
through Joseph Cooper Walker

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:06.640
of another pirated edition of the text,

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.290
but this time in Dublin.

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:10.580
In the meantime, without
Walpole's knowledge,

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:13.320
Lord Charlemont suppressed
this publication

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:15.240
on the aged author's behalf,

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:17.970
and while Walpole remained
enormously grateful

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:19.630
towards his protector,

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:22.510
he had become by this time,

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:26.490
quite exhausted by the entire debacle.

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:28.040
"I have lived too long,

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:30.000
not to have divested myself.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.950
both of vanity, and affected modesty."

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:35.120
Very interesting phrase.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:36.360
Affected modesty.

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:40.250
He writes to Charlemont in February 1971.

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:43.490
"I have not existed to past seventy-three

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:46.110
without having discovered the futility

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:48.530
and triflingness of my own talents--

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:51.480
and at the same time it
would be impertinent,"

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:53.540
[Quick change of rhetorical direction]

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:55.900
"to pretend to think that there is no merit

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:57.840
in the execution of a tragedy,

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:00.380
on which I'd been so much flattered;

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:04.180
though I am sincere in condemning
the egregious absurdity

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.103
of selecting a subject
so improper for the stage

00:23:06.103 --> 00:23:10.382
and even offensive to private readers--"

00:23:10.382 --> 00:23:11.470
Dot, dot, dot.

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:13.730
"I will beg your lordship, if you please,

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:15.750
to signify to the bookseller,

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:18.848
that you withdraw your prohibition."

00:23:18.848 --> 00:23:22.800
Albeit with Walpole's very
begrudgingly granted consent,

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:25.910
"The Mysterious Mother" was
duly published in Dublin,

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:29.436
by Archer, Jones, and White, in 1791.

00:23:29.436 --> 00:23:32.842
Walpole's name only
mentioned in a cited extract

00:23:32.842 --> 00:23:35.230
from a description of the play

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:36.560
that had first been published

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.400
in Biographica Dramatica in 1782.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:41.900
Now a poem which I believe

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:45.110
has been reprinted on the
back of your program for today,

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:46.610
by one Florizel

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:49.003
that was published in "The World,"

00:23:49.003 --> 00:23:52.040
Wednesday the 12th of October, 1791,

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:54.840
indicates something of
the public approbation

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.945
with which the eventual publication

00:23:56.945 --> 00:24:00.120
of this mysterious piece was met.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:03.040
Walpole, for this anonymous poet at least,

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:05.631
had restored the spirits of gothic genius

00:24:05.631 --> 00:24:08.800
that had last been seen
on the English stage

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.170
in Shakespeare's plays.

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:13.200
There's a reprint of the Dublin edition

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.010
that is published in London in 1791,

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:19.090
which this time features Walpole's name

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:20.450
on the title page,

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:23.770
and another edition follows in 1796.

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:26.200
Now after so many years of resistance,

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.134
Walpole consents to the publication

00:24:28.134 --> 00:24:29.780
of The Mysterious Mother

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:32.210
in the first volume of
his posthumous works.

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:33.990
That Berry oversees,

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:35.530
including in volume four,

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:37.670
a hitherto unpublished prologue,

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:40.850
that had been written in 1768.

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:42.830
Now of course if publication is a matter

00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:45.970
of such urgency and anxiety,

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:48.130
performance is inconceivable.

00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:49.430
Although as I've suggested,

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:51.110
there is evidence to suggest

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:54.640
that Walpole did seriously
contemplate its production.

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:56.766
An appreciative man summed up the views

00:24:56.766 --> 00:24:58.690
of Walpole and his milieu,

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:00.970
when he wrote that "the subject may be

00:25:00.970 --> 00:25:02.790
too horrid for the stage,

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:05.070
but the judicious management of it,

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:07.490
by preparing the reader
for the catastrophe.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:09.550
The sublime ideas and expressions

00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:12.380
so admirably adapted to the personages

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:16.025
make it a delicious
entertainment for the closet."

00:25:16.025 --> 00:25:18.450
In Walpole's lifetime, as we know,

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:20.060
it remains a closet drama.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.560
The likes of Goethe's "Faust,"

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.090
or later dramas of Byron and Shelley

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:26.370
and as such is intended only to be read

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:28.040
by a solitary reader,

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:31.270
or sometimes aloud in a small group

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:32.950
variously with or without

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.960
the author's knowledge or consent.

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:38.180
What is intriguing is the many accounts

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:41.450
of these reading experiences that remain.

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:44.850
My favorite is by the Scottish
antiquarian John Pinkerton

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:48.190
who recalls in "Walpoliana" of 1799,

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:51.620
a chilling nighttime
reading of Walpole's tragedy

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:56.460
at Strawberry Hill on the
20th of September 1784.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.850
The occasion on which
Walpole leaves him alone,

00:25:58.850 --> 00:26:01.830
we believe with the script at 11 PM

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:03.740
while he calls upon a neighbor.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:06.430
Now as Walpole and Pinkerton both know,

00:26:06.430 --> 00:26:09.050
the date of this reading, 20th September,

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:11.500
is in itself highly significant,

00:26:11.500 --> 00:26:13.370
because it uncannily marks,

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:16.800
the anniversary of that
night so many years before,

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:18.430
on which the Count of Narbonne

00:26:18.430 --> 00:26:20.870
was gored to death by the wild stag.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:23.090
The night on which the Countess seduced

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:25.180
but then banished her son Edmund

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:27.499
and also the night on which Edmund returns

00:26:27.499 --> 00:26:30.678
to Narbonne exactly 16 years later.

00:26:30.678 --> 00:26:33.950
I think another uncanny coincidence,

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:36.240
that was Walpole's decision,

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:39.190
to call upon Catherine or Kitty Clive

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:42.850
on this night, the 20th of September 1784,

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:44.630
of all nights. As we know.

00:26:44.630 --> 00:26:46.260
it was the Clive,

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:47.970
the famed comic actress who was

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:49.650
to speak the epilogue to the play.

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:51.860
Another decision that betrays I think

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:54.330
Walpole's intentions to see it performed.

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:57.265
From 1753, the Clive had been living

00:26:57.265 --> 00:26:59.310
at Twickenham at Little Marble Hill.

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.790
A property owned by Walpole himself,

00:27:01.790 --> 00:27:05.670
and upon her retirement
from the stage in 1769,

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:07.350
she moved to Little Strawberry Hill,

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:08.900
later known as Cliveden,

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:10.977
another Twickenham property

00:27:10.977 --> 00:27:12.803
within the vicinity of Strawberry Hill

00:27:12.803 --> 00:27:14.410
that Walpole had built in 1768,

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:17.029
and which he generously gifted to her.

00:27:17.029 --> 00:27:20.150
So, 20th September 1784,

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:23.649
Pinkerton reads Walpole's
play at Strawberry Hill

00:27:23.649 --> 00:27:26.940
thus marking the kind of random congruence

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:28.340
of a number of factors

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:31.490
that were intimately related
to Walpole's tragedy.

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:34.564
"This odd circumstance," writes Pinkerton,

00:27:34.564 --> 00:27:37.370
conspired with the complete solitude

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:38.960
of the Gothic apartments,

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.200
to lend an additional impression

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:43.960
to the superstitious
parts of that tragedy.

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:45.140
In point of language,

00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:47.660
and the true expression
of passion and feeling,

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:50.510
the new and just delineation
of monastic fraud,

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:54.640
tyranny and cruelty; it
deserves the greatest praise."

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:57.500
Now of course another well-known record

00:27:57.500 --> 00:27:59.640
of a reader's engagement with the play

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:04.460
comes from the court journals
of Frances Burney, in 1786,

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:07.330
in which Burney recounts her shocked

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:09.810
even traumatized reactions,

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:12.420
to a group reading of the
play at Windsor Castle

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:15.603
merely four months after
her arrival at court.

00:28:16.633 --> 00:28:19.290
Just to pick out a few lines.

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:22.530
"For myself, I felt a sort
of indignant aversion

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:25.520
rise fast and warm in my mind,

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:27.190
against the willful author

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:29.220
of a story so horrible:

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:31.120
all the entertainment and pleasure

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:32.820
I had received from Mr. Walpole

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:35.460
seemed extinguished by his lecture,

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:37.010
which almost made me regard him

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.270
as the patron of the vices

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:41.540
he had been pleased to record."

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:43.410
I think what happens is that

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:46.429
through these and other
such whispered accounts,

00:28:46.429 --> 00:28:49.590
the withheld play achieved something

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:52.220
of a mythological status.

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:54.440
In all his reluctance to share it,

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:57.380
Walpole, the best
selling author of "Otranto"

00:28:57.380 --> 00:29:00.993
has clearly made a mystery
of "The Mysterious Mother."

00:29:02.097 --> 00:29:05.456
And thus we might begin
to track the afterlife,

00:29:05.456 --> 00:29:09.010
of the play in romantic era Britain.

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:13.615
In 1792, in the preface
to her play "The Massacre,"

00:29:13.615 --> 00:29:16.310
the radical writer Elizabeth Inchbald

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.690
claimed that her own decision

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:22.000
to write and publish the play

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:24.420
yet withhold it from production,

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:27.010
was directly informed by the precedent

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:30.250
that Walpole had set in
"The Mysterious Mother."

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:34.166
But to conclude the
influence of Walpole's play,

00:29:34.166 --> 00:29:37.950
goes well beyond the
late 18th century stage.

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:41.540
As a primarily textual
phenomenon in its day,

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:44.220
its natural destination, I think,

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:47.303
were the prose genres
of romantic era fiction.

00:29:48.898 --> 00:29:51.448
I think the play opens up
imaginative possibilities

00:29:54.253 --> 00:29:55.483
that later gothic fiction
would come to exploit.

00:29:56.435 --> 00:29:59.529
For it was in a drama with art which

00:29:59.529 --> 00:30:03.970
such later gothic representations

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:06.060
of brother sister incest,

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:09.890
in say Mathew Lewis's "The Monk" of 1796,

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.890
would not have been possible,

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:15.350
Ann Radcliffe twice used
extracts from the play

00:30:15.350 --> 00:30:19.280
as epigraphs to The Italian of 1797.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:20.870
Reworking the relationship

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:23.263
between the Countess and Father Benedict,

00:30:23.263 --> 00:30:26.390
in that between the Marchesa di Vivaldi

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:29.970
and the notorious villain Father Schedoni.

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:32.930
Later romantics were
equally enamored of it.

00:30:32.930 --> 00:30:35.220
In the preface to Marino Faliero,

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:38.960
Byron praised it as "a
tragedy of the highest order,

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:41.670
and not a puling love play."

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:43.400
Walpole, he continued,

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.346
"is the father of the
first romance [Otranto],

00:30:46.346 --> 00:30:50.000
and of the last tragedy in our language,

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:52.140
and surely worthy of a higher place

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:54.330
than any other living writer,

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.870
be he who he may."

00:30:56.870 --> 00:31:00.460
Walter Scott was altogether
less enthusiastic,

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:02.846
when he anthologized the play,

00:31:02.846 --> 00:31:07.103
in Ballantyne's "Modern
British Drama," in 1811.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:10.760
Later, in "Lives of the Novelists,"

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:15.610
Scott would pass the play
as "a horribly impressive

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:17.799
but disgusting drama."

00:31:17.799 --> 00:31:21.700
Irrespective of whether
it was revered or reviled,

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:24.177
one might sincerely
ponder the extent to which

00:31:24.177 --> 00:31:28.000
some of the greatest
sexually transgressive texts

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:29.950
of canonical romanticism,

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:31.700
and here I'm thinking of Shelley's,

00:31:35.006 --> 00:31:36.059
"Cenci" or "Laon and Cythna,"

00:31:36.059 --> 00:31:37.718
or Mary Shelley's "Mathilda,"

00:31:37.718 --> 00:31:39.350
and especially double incest plot

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.760
of John Polidori's "Ernestus Berchtold,"

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:45.017
possibly even Byron's "Manfred,"

00:31:45.017 --> 00:31:46.890
would have been possible,

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:49.110
without "The Mysterious Mother."

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:52.190
and that cultivated sense of secrecy,

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:54.100
and mystery that surrounded it.

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:54.933
Thank you.

00:31:55.769 --> 00:31:58.769
(audience applause)

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:16.123
- Thank you so much Dale.

00:32:17.510 --> 00:32:20.093
Our next speaker is Matthew M. Reeve.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:23.100
He is Associate Professor of Art History

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:24.610
and Queen's National Scholar,

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:26.433
at Queen's University,

00:32:26.433 --> 00:32:28.930
and a fellow of The
Society of Antiquaries.

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:32.120
Working in both the
medieval and modern periods,

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:34.310
he has just completed
a revisionist account

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:36.960
of gothic architecture, sexuality,

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:40.000
and aesthetics in the
circle of Horace Walpole.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.340
It will be coming out in 2019,

00:32:42.340 --> 00:32:45.790
and it follows from essays
in "The Art Bulletin,"

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:46.983
"Architectural History,"

00:32:46.983 --> 00:32:50.890
"The Burlington Magazine," and other venues.

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:52.170
Matthew will be speaking to us

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:56.001
on "'The Mysterious Mother'
and crypto-Catholicism

00:32:56.001 --> 00:32:58.263
in the Circle of Horace Walpole."

00:33:01.221 --> 00:33:03.117
- Anyway, thank you very
much for having me here

00:33:03.117 --> 00:33:06.500
and to our wonderful support,

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:08.260
and I wanna also thank
really really quickly

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:10.348
The Lewis Walpole Library,

00:33:10.348 --> 00:33:12.870
because they gave me my
start on this project

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.690
in a wonderful way over
a summer fellowship,

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:17.570
and they also taught me how
to play professional croquet.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:18.640
Which was an amazing thing.

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:20.486
Those of you who've been there,

00:33:20.486 --> 00:33:21.473
know exactly what I'm talking about.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:23.830
As you heard this is a special treat

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:24.663
for me to be here,

00:33:24.663 --> 00:33:26.730
because I'm not officially
an English specialist.

00:33:26.730 --> 00:33:28.310
Nor an 18th century-ist, in fact,

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:29.980
but an art historian and a medievalist.

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:32.559
My interest in Walpole
and his homoerotic circle

00:33:32.559 --> 00:33:34.900
of male friends and designers and patrons,

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:37.100
concern their pivotal role in the shaping

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:39.550
of the gothic idiom in the 18th century,

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:40.530
and their art writing,

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:42.620
their art and architectural
patronage particularly,

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:44.920
and in my case, tangentially
their literature.

00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:48.080
Although it's seldom understood as such,

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.460
Walpolian gothic as an historical idiom

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:52.650
confronted and was informed by

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:54.888
its historical roots in
medieval Catholicism.

00:33:54.888 --> 00:33:57.660
In England the official
end of Catholicism,

00:33:57.660 --> 00:34:00.230
with the 16th century
dissolution of the monasteries

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:02.670
established a fundamental
break in religious structures,

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:03.710
and by many accounts,

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:05.610
including that of Horace Walpole,

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:07.663
its heritage in gothic architecture.

00:34:08.709 --> 00:34:11.883
Walpole's gothic reflects a
complex response to Catholicism

00:34:11.883 --> 00:34:14.470
that was variously critical and porodic,

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:16.450
romantic and even erotic,

00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:18.210
but like many goths and antiquaries

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:19.570
before and after him,

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:21.010
it is motivated fundamentally,

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:22.660
if not straight forwardly,

00:34:22.660 --> 00:34:26.752
by mourning for a lost
spiritual and aesthetic past.

00:34:26.752 --> 00:34:28.560
In setting this up I want to say

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:30.550
a little bit first of all about

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:32.160
Walpole's appraisal of Catholicism,

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:34.650
and then ask how this context might inform

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:36.463
a reading of "The Mysterious Mother."

00:34:37.886 --> 00:34:39.770
It goes without saying,

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:41.350
that few men within Walpole's circle,

00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:43.786
were either Catholic,
or Catholic converts.

00:34:43.786 --> 00:34:47.670
They could be more usefully
understood as crypto-Catholics.

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:49.813
Walpole described himself
as a Protestant goth

00:34:49.813 --> 00:34:53.210
and a strain of anti-Catholicism
runs through his writing

00:34:53.210 --> 00:34:55.380
as of course, it does
for the period itself.

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:56.750
Yet he felt no tension,

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:58.620
in writing to Reverend William Cole,

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.840
saying things like I like
potpourri as well as you do

00:35:01.840 --> 00:35:03.193
and I've shown that I do.

00:35:04.504 --> 00:35:06.900
I like it like I like
chivalry and romance.

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:09.190
They all furnish one
with ideas and visions,

00:35:09.190 --> 00:35:11.730
which Presbyterianism does not.

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:13.580
A gothic church or convent,

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:16.750
fills one with romantic dreams.

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:19.100
Walpole playfully
allegorized Strawberry hill

00:35:19.100 --> 00:35:20.413
as a Catholic Shrine,

00:35:21.749 --> 00:35:23.570
calling it a gothic
Vatican of Greece and Rome,

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:25.900
and he was clear that
aspect of its interiors

00:35:25.900 --> 00:35:27.500
were intentional approximations

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:29.140
of the splendor and gaudiness

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:31.520
to use his word, of medieval Catholic art,

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:33.583
with all the glory of potpourri.

00:35:34.436 --> 00:35:36.600
Walpole and his circle actively

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:38.733
and playfully explored the Catholic,

00:35:39.755 --> 00:35:41.137
and quasi-religious nature,

00:35:41.137 --> 00:35:42.150
in the houses that they built.

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:44.783
The Strawberry Hill oratory for example,

00:35:44.783 --> 00:35:46.240
was a functionless exterior prayer niche,

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:47.750
enclosed within iron rails,

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:49.260
with basins for holy water,

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:51.220
and a 15th century Bronze angel.

00:35:51.220 --> 00:35:55.210
Walpole's famous letter
in 1753 to Horace Mann,

00:35:55.210 --> 00:35:57.420
describes his plans for his new monastery

00:35:57.420 --> 00:35:59.247
at Strawberry Hill.

00:35:59.247 --> 00:36:02.820
He says, "I am greatly
impatient for my alter,

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:05.410
and so far from mistrusting its goodness,

00:36:05.410 --> 00:36:07.090
I only fear it will be too good

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:08.600
to expose to the weather,

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:11.490
as I intended to be a
recess in the garden.

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:12.920
I was going to tell you that my house

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:14.230
is so monastic,

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:17.260
that I have a little hall
decked with long lean saints

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:18.827
in lean arched windows,

00:36:18.827 --> 00:36:21.960
and with long columns that
we call the Paraclete,

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:24.588
in memory of the Eloisa's Cloister."

00:36:24.588 --> 00:36:28.240
This is typical of the range
of religious significations,

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:29.530
and frankly, in jokes

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:30.840
that he and his circle shared,

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:32.930
in which Catholicism, its rites,

00:36:32.930 --> 00:36:34.130
settings, and rituals,

00:36:34.130 --> 00:36:36.180
were subject to the highly nuanced

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:38.364
coterie taste and understanding.

00:36:38.364 --> 00:36:40.210
He concludes with a reference

00:36:40.210 --> 00:36:41.890
to the Paraclete or the entrance hall

00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:43.600
as I show it to you here.

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:45.700
The name of which is borrowed from Pope's

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:48.091
"Eloisa and Abelard" of 1717, of course.

00:36:48.091 --> 00:36:50.617
Elsewhere in describing Dicky Bateman.

00:36:50.617 --> 00:36:53.340
His kind of frenemy,
we might say these days,

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:55.785
at his building of his villa

00:36:55.785 --> 00:36:57.780
by the Strawberry Hill designers.

00:36:57.780 --> 00:36:59.430
Walpole claims, and I quote that

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:02.460
"Bateman's villa had changed its religion.

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:03.360
I converted it.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:06.090
--I converted it from Chinese to Gothic,

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:07.880
and that his monkish chambers,

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:10.680
were always dressed as
though in full mourning."

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:11.513
Unquote.

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:14.350
These men would develop
a rich series of tropes

00:37:14.350 --> 00:37:16.840
to describe themselves as brother monks,

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:18.933
abbots, and their houses as nunneries,

00:37:21.202 --> 00:37:22.035
monasteries and so forth.

00:37:22.035 --> 00:37:24.868
They would also perform
their crypto-Catholism,

00:37:24.868 --> 00:37:28.370
through faux liturgies
at the vine in Hampshire,

00:37:28.370 --> 00:37:30.530
with ancient mass books and incense.

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:32.740
Where they had what they
called, quote, unquote,

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:35.430
a most Catholic enjoyment
of the chapel there.

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:38.300
A quotation I'm still
trying to understand.

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:43.300
Walpole could allegorize a
visit to Strawberry Hill

00:37:43.377 --> 00:37:46.360
by George Montagu as the
festival of St. George

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:47.790
and speak of the gorgeous saints

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:49.100
and stained glass he acquired,

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:50.750
and so forth and so on and so on.

00:37:51.724 --> 00:37:54.440
Catholicism and the gothic shared

00:37:54.440 --> 00:37:56.040
a common temporal location,

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:57.450
in Walpole's historiography.

00:37:57.450 --> 00:37:58.760
This is an important point.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:01.750
i.e. variously, up to and even after 1540,

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:03.750
particularly with reference
for literature in Shakespeare

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:04.853
as we heard yesterday.

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:07.810
For Walpole, both were associated

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:09.760
with a form of sensory, ethical,

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:11.160
and labidonal freedom,

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:13.420
what he called visionary enjoyment.

00:38:13.420 --> 00:38:15.240
A state of being he felt was diminished,

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:17.810
by the extinction of Catholicism,

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:20.170
chivalry, of the gothic style itself.

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:22.590
With the dissolution of the monasteries.

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:25.030
As an historical moment
of rupture and change,

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:27.930
he considers the dissolution
in Protestantism itself

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:30.650
to reflect a reform of human emotional

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:32.300
and intellectual aesthesis.

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:34.120
Reform, in other words,
was not a good thing,

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:35.193
for Horace Walpole.

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:38.390
In architecture, the classicism

00:38:38.390 --> 00:38:40.640
that followed the
dissolution was replaced.

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:41.473
Pardon me.

00:38:41.473 --> 00:38:43.725
Dissolution replaced the gothic

00:38:43.725 --> 00:38:44.558
in Walpole's developmental theory.

00:38:44.558 --> 00:38:46.420
In other words, the English Renaissance.

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:48.596
and this was understood by him

00:38:48.596 --> 00:38:50.400
as a destructive reform
of the built environment.

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:53.670
It was the result of stifled
human aesthetic agency.

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:55.470
Of creativity being hemmed in

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:57.510
by the oppressive rules of classicism,

00:38:57.510 --> 00:38:59.320
or reform or Protestantism,

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:01.750
as his argument follows.

00:39:01.750 --> 00:39:03.770
The gothic becomes a style and a period,

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:05.470
of unrestrained creativity.

00:39:05.470 --> 00:39:07.140
A space for the free unfiltered,

00:39:07.140 --> 00:39:09.440
and unregulated projection
of the imagination.

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:12.200
As such, a revival of the gothic,

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:14.290
meant not simply return to the forms

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:15.960
of later medieval architecture,

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:18.370
but also to its spiritual
and performative modes,

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:20.340
or at least particularly specific 

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:22.400
and camp even conception of them,

00:39:22.400 --> 00:39:24.920
and of course in this he has his critics.

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:26.570
Madame du Deffand would respond to

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:28.430
"The Mysterious Mother," by stating,

00:39:28.430 --> 00:39:30.840
I do not like Abelard and Eloise at all.

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:33.210
Something in this case,
she connects to the play.

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:34.570
Whether ancient or modern,

00:39:34.570 --> 00:39:36.180
her endearments, her passions,

00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:38.550
are not my style.

00:39:38.550 --> 00:39:40.530
Walpole responds noting that the play

00:39:40.530 --> 00:39:42.980
is presented as a kind of gothic,

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:44.080
which would not be found in your,

00:39:44.080 --> 00:39:46.370
in other words French theater.

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:48.190
A kind of polite dropping of the mic

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:49.323
if there ever was one.

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:53.620
As a subversive other mode

00:39:53.620 --> 00:39:55.440
of spiritual and political regions

00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:57.080
within parts of England,

00:39:57.080 --> 00:39:59.270
Catholicism and non normative sexuality

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:02.340
were marked by a history of
tropological substitution

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:05.590
and inter implication for
contemporary audiences.

00:40:05.590 --> 00:40:08.360
Offering the fantasy
of monosexual culture.

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:10.830
Many of the gothicisms
in Walpole's circle,

00:40:10.830 --> 00:40:12.430
suggest queer appropriations

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:14.470
of Catholic rituals and aesthetics.

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:16.480
Beneath the cowl of monastacism

00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:19.123
was a cult of male homo erotic community.

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:22.530
Indeed the cloister had
been usefully understood

00:40:22.530 --> 00:40:24.120
as a preparatory space,

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:25.920
for the closet in modern constructions

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:28.303
of homosexuality in recent research.

00:40:29.556 --> 00:40:31.140
So much is clear,

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:32.290
but to nuance this point.

00:40:32.290 --> 00:40:34.175
It does need a lot of nuance,

00:40:34.175 --> 00:40:36.810
monasticism was also used as a guise

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:39.290
to perform overtly heterosexual license

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:41.180
as in the case of sir Francis Dashwood

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:42.440
and the Medmenham Monks,

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:44.440
just up the Thames from Strawberry Hill,

00:40:44.440 --> 00:40:47.160
who performed an orgiastic
exploits with nuns.

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:49.560
Quote, unquote, nuns,
within gothic settings.

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:51.800
The so-called Hellfire caves,

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:53.190
and we can of course trace

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:55.157
such an association between Catholicism

00:40:55.157 --> 00:40:58.140
and alternate or unreformed sexuality,

00:40:58.140 --> 00:41:00.950
across many cultural
productions contemporaneously.

00:41:00.950 --> 00:41:03.080
Including this newly acquired print here,

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.750
at The Lewis Walpole Library.

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:06.290
Thanks to Cindy for sending this.

00:41:06.290 --> 00:41:07.903
I just got this a week ago.

00:41:11.250 --> 00:41:12.083
Walpole's use of gothic,

00:41:12.083 --> 00:41:13.470
to describe "The Mysterious Mother"

00:41:14.527 --> 00:41:15.470
and his own crypto-Catholic
practices aside,

00:41:15.470 --> 00:41:16.820
such as they may be,

00:41:16.820 --> 00:41:19.150
what does this have to do with the play?

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:21.013
Well, superficially at least,

00:41:22.054 --> 00:41:23.631
the continental gothic past,

00:41:23.631 --> 00:41:25.364
or Catholic past, excuse me,

00:41:25.364 --> 00:41:27.026
becomes a space that normalizes,

00:41:27.026 --> 00:41:28.189
or at least makes possible

00:41:28.189 --> 00:41:29.440
radical sexual difference.

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:31.680
It facilitates the
story's central narrative

00:41:32.721 --> 00:41:34.810
of incest as a Freudian family romance,

00:41:34.810 --> 00:41:37.820
safely removed from a
contemporary English context,

00:41:37.820 --> 00:41:40.010
and here I'm recalling George Haggerty's

00:41:40.010 --> 00:41:41.430
remarkable observation,

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:43.640
that the monastery
functions as a precursor

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:46.080
to the laboratory in Georgian culture.

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:49.483
As a space to explore and
examine sexual difference.

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:53.150
The play's overt focus on Catholicism,

00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:56.620
its rights, its apparently
villainous cleric associations,

00:41:56.620 --> 00:41:58.640
has stood at the center
of the play's reception

00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:01.490
from Paul Baines's view of Horace Walpole,

00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:05.600
what he calls "a manifestly anti-sacredotal focus."

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:06.570
Excuse me.

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:09.870
To a 1797 critique which
stated that, and I quote,

00:42:09.870 --> 00:42:12.150
Mr. Walpole was a bitter foe to priests,

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:13.900
without distinction,

00:42:13.900 --> 00:42:15.930
and seems to have had no great reverence

00:42:15.930 --> 00:42:17.320
for sacred mysteries,

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:19.993
or the doctrinal parts,
of the Christian system.

00:42:21.115 --> 00:42:23.780
But I want to follow Walpole here himself,

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:25.250
in his famous postscript,

00:42:25.250 --> 00:42:27.088
in which he says, I quote,

00:42:27.088 --> 00:42:29.460
"the villainy of Benedict was planned

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:31.940
to divide the indignation of the audience,

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:35.200
and to intercept some
of it from the Countess.

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:37.780
Nor will the blackness of this
character appear extravagant

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:39.510
if we call to mind the crimes

00:42:39.510 --> 00:42:41.360
committed by Catholic Churchman,

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:44.313
when the reformation, not
only provoked their rage,

00:42:45.466 --> 00:42:47.266
but threatened them with total ruin."

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:50.040
While the play clearly exploits

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:51.510
the writing cliches of villanous

00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:53.910
or licentious Catholic clergy,

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:56.280
Walpole goes somewhere to historicize,

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:58.980
and even justify Benedict's actions.

00:42:58.980 --> 00:43:00.620
In this passage the Monks really are

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:02.683
the victims of a changing tide.

00:43:03.768 --> 00:43:05.970
In one sense the play turns,

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:07.740
on the thwarting eventual demise

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:10.710
of Catholicism's very structures.

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:13.750
In this sense Narbonne is
like T.S. Elliot's "Wasteland,"

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:16.180
suffering a period of
moral and physical decline

00:43:16.180 --> 00:43:19.143
after the transgression
of 16 years previous.

00:43:19.143 --> 00:43:21.180
The Countess has largely neglected

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:23.590
her traditional role as
a benefactor to the abby

00:43:23.590 --> 00:43:26.030
and as a result it is partially ruinous.

00:43:26.030 --> 00:43:27.330
But more importantly

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:28.840
as Benedict understands it,

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:30.520
she has neglected her spiritual role

00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:33.000
to her priest and confessor.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.860
Benedict continually reaches out to her,

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:36.540
but she shuns and mocks him,

00:43:36.540 --> 00:43:39.913
knowing the church could never
correct or contain her sin.

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:42.460
Here the often cited parallel

00:43:42.460 --> 00:43:44.140
between the medieval confessional

00:43:45.509 --> 00:43:48.536
as a precursor to the modern
psychiatrist sofa seems apt.

00:43:48.536 --> 00:43:49.369
Since religion grandly declines,

00:43:49.369 --> 00:43:50.830
and is even replaced,

00:43:50.830 --> 00:43:52.220
with the rise of psychoanalysis,

00:43:52.220 --> 00:43:54.178
to follow one narrative anyway.

00:43:54.178 --> 00:43:56.230
Having for all intents and purposes,

00:43:56.230 --> 00:43:57.480
fired her psychiatrist,

00:43:57.480 --> 00:43:59.830
in the sense the Countess has disrupted

00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:02.060
the traditional
epistemological relationship

00:44:02.060 --> 00:44:03.890
between the family and their faith.

00:44:03.890 --> 00:44:06.538
Thus upsetting the structures of knowledge

00:44:06.538 --> 00:44:08.260
and power medieval Catholicism.

00:44:08.260 --> 00:44:09.730
As a result it is the castle,

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:10.940
and not the monastery

00:44:10.940 --> 00:44:12.370
that becomes the physical container

00:44:12.370 --> 00:44:14.840
and site for the exploration of trauma,

00:44:14.840 --> 00:44:16.833
unmediated by Catholic confession.

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:20.310
This might help us to understand

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:22.120
why Walpole locates the plot,

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:24.340
in a liminal space of the reformation.

00:44:24.340 --> 00:44:25.990
Rather than the period of the crusades,

00:44:25.990 --> 00:44:27.840
as in Otranto.

00:44:27.840 --> 00:44:29.910
The reformation, not only historicizes

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:32.160
the actions of the captain clergy,

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:34.550
but it thematizes them as well.

00:44:34.550 --> 00:44:36.240
And so too does it position

00:44:37.861 --> 00:44:38.743
the Countess's very subjectivity,

00:44:39.987 --> 00:44:41.336
between an exterior piety,

00:44:41.336 --> 00:44:42.709
constant with Catholic confession,

00:44:42.709 --> 00:44:44.360
and an interior redemptive piety,

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:45.980
consistent with Protestantism,

00:44:45.980 --> 00:44:48.180
and as Dale and I discussed last night,

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:49.590
perhaps she was already at a process

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:50.953
of conversion herself.

00:44:52.955 --> 00:44:55.480
If I'm hinting here at
a strain of apology,

00:44:55.480 --> 00:44:56.780
or something like apology,

00:44:56.780 --> 00:44:57.940
in Walpole's construction,

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:00.470
of the Catholic in gothic past,

00:45:00.470 --> 00:45:02.306
I do so for a good reason.

00:45:02.306 --> 00:45:05.090
Walpole and the medievalists
of his generation,

00:45:05.090 --> 00:45:07.010
inherited an historiographical tradition

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:09.620
from the early modern
antiquarian predecessors.

00:45:09.620 --> 00:45:12.690
People like John Stowe, and
William Dugdale and others,

00:45:12.690 --> 00:45:15.020
that look to the dissolution
as a fundamental break,

00:45:15.020 --> 00:45:17.470
in the religious and social
character of a country,

00:45:17.470 --> 00:45:18.910
and yearned for a return,

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:21.940
to the predisolution of Catholic past.

00:45:21.940 --> 00:45:23.703
Walpole absorbed these ideas,

00:45:24.624 --> 00:45:25.700
and made them central to his home.

00:45:25.700 --> 00:45:28.510
He even used various now lost monuments.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:30.480
Illustrated in Dugdale's text,

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:33.400
to become the gothic
installations at Strawberry Hill,

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:36.270
thereby imprinting to
use Walpole's own word,

00:45:36.270 --> 00:45:38.940
this nostalgic vision of the Catholic past

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:40.480
upon the inside of this famous home.

00:45:40.480 --> 00:45:42.130
And I just gave you a second example here,

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:44.380
that's a little bit more
pleasant to look at.

00:45:46.242 --> 00:45:48.479
Now Walpole's crypto-Catholicism,

00:45:48.479 --> 00:45:49.630
was of course a tangled ambivalent aspect

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:50.970
of his historicism.

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:53.060
Of his very nostalgia.

00:45:53.060 --> 00:45:54.230
He could for example,

00:45:54.230 --> 00:45:55.730
offer a lament upon seeing

00:45:55.730 --> 00:45:57.710
Kings College Chapel at Cambridge.

00:45:57.710 --> 00:45:59.110
Again, after some years,

00:45:59.110 --> 00:46:01.010
he says that penetrated me

00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:04.530
with a visionary longing
to be a monk in it.

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:05.980
A pretty odd comment to be sure,

00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:08.910
for a collegiate rather
than a monastic foundation.

00:46:08.910 --> 00:46:10.450
But here as elsewhere in all of this,

00:46:10.450 --> 00:46:13.230
I think we fail at Walpole
at taking him literally

00:46:13.230 --> 00:46:15.850
and reducing his writings
to legalistic documents

00:46:15.850 --> 00:46:18.550
that prove or not specific ideas,

00:46:18.550 --> 00:46:21.323
rather than interrogating
them with his own language.

00:46:23.460 --> 00:46:26.180
Now I want to close with a
passage from a wonderful book,

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:28.050
Michael Anne Holly's recent book,

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:29.500
"The Melancholy Art."

00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:31.470
In which she describes the art historian's

00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:32.840
own subjective position,

00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:35.290
against her own historical subject.

00:46:35.290 --> 00:46:36.700
A passage that I think,

00:46:36.700 --> 00:46:39.540
might usefully gloss
Walpole's own historicism

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:41.500
vis-à-vis the Catholic past,

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:46.350
and she says nostalgia is simply
not capacious enough a word

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:48.890
to name the source of the sensation.

00:46:48.890 --> 00:46:51.410
This homesickness of things.

00:46:51.410 --> 00:46:53.410
What we momentarily encounter there,

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:57.980
is the living pulsating
afterlife of the past."

00:46:57.980 --> 00:46:59.259
Thank you.

00:46:59.259 --> 00:47:02.259
(audience applause)

00:47:11.260 --> 00:47:12.563
- Thank you Matthew.

00:47:13.550 --> 00:47:15.480
Our next speaker is Nicole Garret.

00:47:15.480 --> 00:47:17.160
She's a part time faculty member at

00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:19.530
SUNY Stonybrook and Adelphi University,

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:23.123
and a recent Lewis Walpole Library Fellow.

00:47:26.232 --> 00:47:28.613
Nicole has co-edited two
novels of Frances Sheridan's.

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:31.871
Sidney Bidulph novels

00:47:31.871 --> 00:47:33.620
that are available through Broadview.

00:47:33.620 --> 00:47:35.690
and also coauthored several essays

00:47:35.690 --> 00:47:39.860
on 19th century religious enthusiasm.

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:41.340
Dr. Garret's current research

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:43.810
is on literary and cultural narratives

00:47:43.810 --> 00:47:46.561
of maternal grief and
discourses of consolation

00:47:46.561 --> 00:47:49.180
in the long 18th century.

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:50.930
Her paper today is titled,

00:47:50.930 --> 00:47:53.863
"Misreading in 'The Mysterious Mother'."

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:00.023
- Thank you.

00:48:00.960 --> 00:48:03.010
I don't know what makes me more nervous.

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:04.630
This room or this microphone.

00:48:04.630 --> 00:48:08.190
I want to thank of course,

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:09.993
The Lewis Walpole Library,

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:13.250
and Cindy Roman, and all of the support

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:16.090
that you've given me over
the past two years or so.

00:48:16.090 --> 00:48:19.440
I will never forget and
never stop coming back,

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:20.803
as long as I'm invited.

00:48:23.764 --> 00:48:26.520
"The Mysterious Mother" is a
funny play in several ways.

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:28.410
As Horace Walpole was fond of saying,

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:30.360
"the world is a comedy to those that think,

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:32.660
and a tragedy to those that feel,"

00:48:32.660 --> 00:48:34.400
and his play does seem to be a reflection

00:48:34.400 --> 00:48:36.470
of the world as he saw it.

00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:39.590
It may be easy to miss the
comedic aspects of the play

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:41.040
especially in re-reading,

00:48:41.040 --> 00:48:42.913
because there's so much to wonder at.

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:47.310
I'm sorry, there's so much to wonder at,

00:48:47.310 --> 00:48:52.310
in the subject of the mother son incest.

00:48:52.950 --> 00:48:54.980
The comedy, like the tragedy,

00:48:54.980 --> 00:48:57.070
is a product of characters misreading,

00:48:57.070 --> 00:48:58.670
both signs in nature,

00:48:58.670 --> 00:49:01.020
and The Mysterious Mother herself.

00:49:01.020 --> 00:49:03.760
In a letter to William Mason in 1769,

00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:06.210
Walpole stated his aim was to, quote,

00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:08.777
present a character whose penitence

00:49:08.777 --> 00:49:11.290
was not degraded by superstitious bigotry.

00:49:11.290 --> 00:49:12.523
Unquote.

00:49:12.523 --> 00:49:14.076
Of course William Mason wanted him

00:49:14.076 --> 00:49:15.610
to try to change the fact that,

00:49:15.610 --> 00:49:17.581
change his character's deliberate

00:49:17.581 --> 00:49:19.770
going to bed with her son,

00:49:19.770 --> 00:49:22.396
and make it a little bit more palatable.

00:49:22.396 --> 00:49:26.780
In that sense, Walpole needed to expose

00:49:26.780 --> 00:49:28.630
and satirize bigotries and pieties

00:49:28.630 --> 00:49:31.300
that generate comic or tragic misreadings,

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:33.170
so when the monks Martin and Benedict,

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:34.840
shudder at a funeral dirge,

00:49:34.840 --> 00:49:36.823
thinking it's a voice beyond the grave,

00:49:37.994 --> 00:49:40.540
saying forebear, we laugh,

00:49:40.540 --> 00:49:42.440
but when others misread
the Countess as a mother

00:49:42.440 --> 00:49:44.900
whose supposedly natural
maternal instincts

00:49:44.900 --> 00:49:47.270
have become corrupted by meddling priests,

00:49:47.270 --> 00:49:49.330
the effect can be tragic.

00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:50.570
What I'd like to do in my short time,

00:49:50.570 --> 00:49:53.660
is look at the function of
bigotry as an interpretive lens.

00:49:53.660 --> 00:49:56.040
How it in effect shields the Countess,

00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:57.450
from discovery of her sins,

00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:59.820
and finally how the Beauclerk drawings

00:49:59.820 --> 00:50:01.380
express Walpole's themes,

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:03.460
without testing the reader's tolerance

00:50:03.460 --> 00:50:04.770
for mother son incest,

00:50:04.770 --> 00:50:05.680
or what might be called

00:50:05.680 --> 00:50:08.440
our bigotries and pieties.

00:50:08.440 --> 00:50:10.300
In the beginning, "The Mysterious Mother"

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:12.450
is mysterious because she's improper.

00:50:12.450 --> 00:50:13.530
She's a bad mother.

00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:15.100
She defies expectations,

00:50:15.100 --> 00:50:17.270
and she fails to provide for needs.

00:50:17.270 --> 00:50:19.443
Nobody really knows the Countess.

00:50:19.443 --> 00:50:21.453
We don't even learn her first name.

00:50:21.453 --> 00:50:23.270
Usually she has no confidant,

00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:25.050
or lady in waiting.

00:50:25.050 --> 00:50:28.693
Her reputation is the product
of rumor and misreading.

00:50:29.560 --> 00:50:31.410
Benedict is her would be confessor.

00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:33.330
At first only knows her enough

00:50:33.330 --> 00:50:35.550
to know he can't quite figure her out.

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:36.970
Her interpreters either read her

00:50:36.970 --> 00:50:38.420
as a hardened heretic,

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:41.660
or having an over abundance
of feminine piety.

00:50:41.660 --> 00:50:43.890
Furthermore they see her
relationship to her son

00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:46.113
as symptomatic of some religious state.

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:49.941
Edmund's friend Florian for instance,

00:50:49.941 --> 00:50:51.400
is certain that, quote, religious mummery,

00:50:51.400 --> 00:50:53.020
and rank fanaticism,

00:50:53.020 --> 00:50:55.820
is the cause of her
separation from her son

00:50:55.820 --> 00:50:59.070
and he denies any sort
of moderation to women.

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:00.580
I never knew a woman but loved

00:51:00.580 --> 00:51:03.097
our bodies or our souls too well.

00:51:03.097 --> 00:51:04.970
That was one of the great
lines from last night.

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:07.850
He sees her as a quote, ruthless mother,

00:51:07.850 --> 00:51:09.470
lacking affection for her son,

00:51:09.470 --> 00:51:12.200
while giving away all
his riches to the church,

00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:13.680
and when Peter the Porter speaks

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:16.170
of his lady's ever heaven directed eye,

00:51:16.170 --> 00:51:18.130
he's giving support to the idea,

00:51:18.130 --> 00:51:20.720
that maternal feeling was spent somehow

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:22.023
in religious devotion.

00:51:23.399 --> 00:51:27.780
Florian's pieties about
motherhood as an affective state

00:51:27.780 --> 00:51:29.614
do not allow him to see that the Countess

00:51:29.614 --> 00:51:33.300
does not need to be
rescued from her monks.

00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:35.560
The first scene between Florian and Peter,

00:51:35.560 --> 00:51:37.350
raises the pressing question,

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:39.370
just how religious is she?

00:51:39.370 --> 00:51:41.850
And just how is she religious?

00:51:41.850 --> 00:51:43.550
The monk Martin, is another one,

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:45.003
attached to his dichotomies.

00:51:46.219 --> 00:51:47.340
In contrast to Florian,

00:51:47.340 --> 00:51:50.210
Martin reads the Countess's refusal

00:51:50.210 --> 00:51:53.060
to take confession, believe
in prophetic visions,

00:51:53.060 --> 00:51:57.070
or take solace in
incantations as rank heresy.

00:51:57.070 --> 00:51:59.960
Benedict however realizes
that simple dichotomies.

00:51:59.960 --> 00:52:02.469
Mother, monster, saint or heretic,

00:52:02.469 --> 00:52:05.280
don't accurately explain her behavior.

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:06.420
She thinks too much.

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:07.980
Is too much a logician,

00:52:07.980 --> 00:52:10.663
and these he says, are pagan virtues.

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:13.710
Although he is determined
to use it to bad ends,

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:15.840
Benedict recognizes that
religious difference

00:52:15.840 --> 00:52:17.220
exists on a spectrum,

00:52:17.220 --> 00:52:18.530
and the Countess confronts him,

00:52:18.530 --> 00:52:19.820
with aspects of the pagan,

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:21.553
and the Christian.

00:52:21.553 --> 00:52:22.810
The devotee, and the heretic,

00:52:22.810 --> 00:52:24.931
as she reasons through her own perdition.

00:52:24.931 --> 00:52:28.000
Because she's less mysterious to him,

00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:29.320
she's more malleable.

00:52:29.320 --> 00:52:31.060
He can manipulate her impulses,

00:52:31.060 --> 00:52:32.770
and has seen glimmers of success

00:52:32.770 --> 00:52:36.130
in that his prophecies
sometimes haunt her dreams.

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:38.290
Peter the Porter has seen her walk around,

00:52:38.290 --> 00:52:41.180
quote with frantic
gesture in a hurried step.

00:52:41.180 --> 00:52:43.330
The philosopher Countess it seems

00:52:43.330 --> 00:52:46.343
has lapses for which she
duly chastises herself.

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:51.010
Subtending the dialogue between
the Countess in Benedict,

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:53.280
many of those scenes we
didn't get to see last night,

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:55.800
probably because they
would have put us to sleep,

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:58.960
are echoes of Hume's
"Natural History of Religion,"

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:01.730
in which he posits that
primitive humans progressed

00:53:01.730 --> 00:53:04.273
from polytheism to monotheism,

00:53:04.273 --> 00:53:06.781
as they turned to contemplating
the natural order,

00:53:06.781 --> 00:53:09.240
rather than the relationship,

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:11.810
between aberrations in the natural world

00:53:11.810 --> 00:53:13.263
and its relationship to life.

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:16.320
In other words, the
practice of interpreting

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:19.980
natural phenomena as
harbingers of events of life,

00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:21.700
is a primitive impulse.

00:53:21.700 --> 00:53:23.440
However, since neither polytheism,

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:25.863
nor monotheism are inherently rational,

00:53:27.915 --> 00:53:29.530
humans may slip back into the
quote, superstitious bigotries

00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:33.240
that we see very much on
display in Walpole's tragedy.

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:35.840
"The Mysterious Mother" is always in danger

00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:37.890
of slipping back into superstition,

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:39.600
and into the momentary madness

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:41.900
that led her to bed her son.

00:53:41.900 --> 00:53:43.890
Later in the play, when racked by grief,

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:45.270
she thinks she sees her husband

00:53:45.270 --> 00:53:46.740
when she beholds her son.

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:48.390
She hears ghosts.

00:53:48.390 --> 00:53:49.830
She believes herself dead.

00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:52.660
She asks for spells to relieve her misery.

00:53:52.660 --> 00:53:55.470
At other times, she scoffs
heroically at Benedict

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:58.540
and bests him with logic and biting wit.

00:53:58.540 --> 00:54:01.630
In short, she wavers,
traveling the whole spectrum,

00:54:01.630 --> 00:54:05.140
between skepticism and
frenzied enthusiasm.

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:08.170
Walpole succeeds in presenting a character

00:54:08.170 --> 00:54:11.280
whose penitence is not degraded
by superstitious bigotry

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:14.190
in part by dramatizing her resistance.

00:54:14.190 --> 00:54:19.100
She says, quote, only weak
minds want their soul's fortune

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:21.620
told by oracles and holy jugglers.

00:54:21.620 --> 00:54:22.520
Unquote.

00:54:22.520 --> 00:54:25.150
Her religious opinions are
ones she's reasoned into

00:54:25.150 --> 00:54:26.530
because of affliction,

00:54:26.530 --> 00:54:27.890
regarding her charity work.

00:54:27.890 --> 00:54:30.965
She also says, quote, "I
did not feel for others.

00:54:30.965 --> 00:54:32.970
I did not think to feel for others,

00:54:32.970 --> 00:54:34.979
because I did not think."

00:54:34.979 --> 00:54:37.590
By the end of Act I, it's established,

00:54:37.590 --> 00:54:39.420
that the Countess's religious positions

00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:40.900
and opinions on priestcraft

00:54:40.900 --> 00:54:42.930
are far from what they're reputed to be,

00:54:42.930 --> 00:54:44.840
and that her freedom from superstition,

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:47.670
depends on maintaining a
certain mental fortitude.

00:54:47.670 --> 00:54:49.170
Her parental behavior,

00:54:49.170 --> 00:54:52.420
operates on a similar parallel spectrum.

00:54:52.420 --> 00:54:54.380
The Countess's relationship to her son,

00:54:54.380 --> 00:54:56.970
is not, as the other characters believe,

00:54:56.970 --> 00:54:59.040
dictated by her religious zealotry,

00:54:59.040 --> 00:55:00.770
or relationship to priests,

00:55:00.770 --> 00:55:03.603
who as she says quail before her.

00:55:04.530 --> 00:55:07.400
Toni Bowers' term, effective motherhood,

00:55:07.400 --> 00:55:10.690
and terms effective motherhood,
and economic motherhood,

00:55:10.690 --> 00:55:12.760
are useful starting points

00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:14.900
for understanding the
Countess's maternal behavior.

00:55:14.900 --> 00:55:17.470
Effective motherhood
being unavailable to her.

00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:19.264
That is the feeling, the nurturing,

00:55:19.264 --> 00:55:21.400
the hugging and the kissing.

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:22.980
She acts our her parental role

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:26.280
rather as a steward of her
son's ancestral wealth.

00:55:26.280 --> 00:55:28.810
Benedict reads this behavior as loveless.

00:55:28.810 --> 00:55:30.670
He says, she loves him not.

00:55:30.670 --> 00:55:32.395
Yet mistress of his fortunes.

00:55:32.395 --> 00:55:35.310
She loves him not,

00:55:35.310 --> 00:55:36.590
yet mistress of his fortunes.

00:55:36.590 --> 00:55:39.020
His ample exhibition speaks her bounty.

00:55:39.020 --> 00:55:41.070
She destines him whatever
her father's love

00:55:41.070 --> 00:55:43.070
gave blindly to her will.

00:55:43.070 --> 00:55:45.180
Edmund still believing
that he was banished

00:55:45.180 --> 00:55:46.580
for an affair with Beatrice,

00:55:46.580 --> 00:55:48.140
calls his mother rigid,

00:55:48.140 --> 00:55:50.440
but denies she's squandering his money.

00:55:50.440 --> 00:55:53.470
He says, to me her hand is as bounteous,

00:55:53.470 --> 00:55:54.963
as her heart is cold.

00:55:56.229 --> 00:55:58.140
Walpole's play therefore
takes up questions

00:55:58.140 --> 00:55:59.990
of maternal difference that are present

00:55:59.990 --> 00:56:02.080
in other mother fictions of the period.

00:56:02.080 --> 00:56:04.030
Defoe's "Roxana" for instance,

00:56:04.030 --> 00:56:06.450
also reasons that economic motherhood.

00:56:06.450 --> 00:56:08.833
that is caring for the
material needs of the child,

00:56:08.833 --> 00:56:13.190
is a meat substitute for
affection and nurturing.

00:56:13.190 --> 00:56:15.170
Neither Roxana nor the Countess,

00:56:15.170 --> 00:56:17.610
are able to tend to their children,

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:20.030
or love them in conventional ways,

00:56:20.030 --> 00:56:22.490
and both find their religious
systems of their birth,

00:56:22.490 --> 00:56:25.230
to be insufficient for
supporting their experiences,

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:27.910
or bearing them up under trial.

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:30.410
Where Roxana works out
biblical interpretations

00:56:30.410 --> 00:56:32.680
that support her peculiar
brand of motherhood,

00:56:32.680 --> 00:56:35.700
Walpole's Countess assumes
the role of the father.

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:38.550
The ruler of lands, money, and children,

00:56:38.550 --> 00:56:41.283
as Benedict calls her,
Mistress of Fortunes.

00:56:42.220 --> 00:56:44.870
Economic stewardship might feel unloving,

00:56:44.870 --> 00:56:47.150
and Edmund certainly feels that it is,

00:56:47.150 --> 00:56:48.810
but the Countess has earned his respect

00:56:48.810 --> 00:56:52.340
through behaviors more immediately
associated with fathers.

00:56:52.340 --> 00:56:54.770
Like a medieval or early
modern Chesterfield,

00:56:54.770 --> 00:56:56.840
the Countess writes letters to her son,

00:56:56.840 --> 00:56:58.400
teaching him how to be a man.

00:56:58.400 --> 00:57:00.700
These letters seem to
countermand her reputation

00:57:00.700 --> 00:57:03.170
as a preying Magdalene.

00:57:03.170 --> 00:57:05.850
Edmund says, quote, her letters Florian,

00:57:05.850 --> 00:57:09.520
such unstudied strains
of virtuous eloquence.

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:10.720
This praying Magdalene,

00:57:10.720 --> 00:57:12.550
enjoins my courage to emulate

00:57:12.550 --> 00:57:14.600
my great forefather's deeds.

00:57:14.600 --> 00:57:17.470
Tell me that shame and
guilt alone are mortal,

00:57:17.470 --> 00:57:19.058
and death but bars the possibility

00:57:19.058 --> 00:57:22.860
of frailty and embalms on tainted honor.

00:57:22.860 --> 00:57:23.693
Unquote.

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:26.410
In reaffirming his banishment,

00:57:26.410 --> 00:57:29.345
she enjoins him to obey her commands,

00:57:29.345 --> 00:57:31.250
and she speaks of her masculine soul.

00:57:31.250 --> 00:57:33.910
She has overall little
respect for mothers.

00:57:33.910 --> 00:57:36.610
Seeing them as dispensable, or worse.

00:57:36.610 --> 00:57:38.170
Trust me she tells Florian,

00:57:38.170 --> 00:57:39.360
a father's guardian arm,

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:41.660
were well worth all the
treasures it withheld.

00:57:43.298 --> 00:57:44.260
A mother might be spared.

00:57:44.260 --> 00:57:45.093
Indeed.

00:57:46.120 --> 00:57:48.030
For the Countess motherhood is corruptible

00:57:48.030 --> 00:57:49.290
and has brought her misery,

00:57:49.290 --> 00:57:50.950
but fatherhood seems possible,

00:57:50.950 --> 00:57:53.400
straightforward, indispensable even,

00:57:53.400 --> 00:57:55.910
and yet the mother cannot be spared.

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:57.280
Both Edmund and Adeliza,

00:57:57.280 --> 00:57:59.270
long for maternal affection.

00:57:59.270 --> 00:58:00.730
For her part although she cannot

00:58:00.730 --> 00:58:02.210
lavish Edmund with affection,

00:58:02.210 --> 00:58:03.610
the Countess is overcome with

00:58:03.610 --> 00:58:05.630
protective emotion at the sight of him.

00:58:05.630 --> 00:58:07.790
Desperate to shield him and Adeliza

00:58:07.790 --> 00:58:09.343
from the knowledge of her sins.

00:58:10.296 --> 00:58:11.129
Overcome with guilt,

00:58:11.129 --> 00:58:13.550
the pull toward effective
motherhood moves separately,

00:58:13.550 --> 00:58:15.350
but in tandem with her backsliding

00:58:15.350 --> 00:58:18.160
into superstition and madness.

00:58:18.160 --> 00:58:21.360
Georgina Locke had a great line last night

00:58:21.360 --> 00:58:23.280
that did come through in the play.

00:58:23.280 --> 00:58:24.113
"Loved him.

00:58:24.113 --> 00:58:26.250
oh nature, bleeding at my heart."

00:58:26.250 --> 00:58:27.840
The nature is pulling at her.

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:30.660
Just by the words, loved him.

00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:33.210
Being asked if she loves him.

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:36.640
The Countess resists certain
kinds of natural impulses

00:58:36.640 --> 00:58:39.070
both to superstition and to affect,

00:58:39.070 --> 00:58:42.040
only for as long as she
can remain mysterious.

00:58:42.040 --> 00:58:45.350
As long as she seems something
unnatural or other worldly,

00:58:45.350 --> 00:58:47.830
and the effect of her natural
passions remains hidden

00:58:47.830 --> 00:58:49.810
and the effect of her natural passions

00:58:49.810 --> 00:58:52.930
remains hidden and
insulated from manipulation.

00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:56.400
Like those who experienced
the 1750 London earthquakes,

00:58:56.400 --> 00:59:00.810
the absurdities of whom Walpole
wrote about shortly after,

00:59:00.810 --> 00:59:02.910
the shock of terrible events,

00:59:02.910 --> 00:59:04.921
separates her from her reason.

00:59:04.921 --> 00:59:07.410
Reason is no match for an earthquake.

00:59:07.410 --> 00:59:09.240
Shared themes of oscillation,

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:11.160
between reason and superstition,

00:59:11.160 --> 00:59:14.860
light and dark, are what
Diana Beauclerk's drawings,

00:59:14.860 --> 00:59:16.620
why they're such fitting illustrations

00:59:16.620 --> 00:59:18.220
for Walpole's play.

00:59:18.220 --> 00:59:21.000
In her drawings some of
what you've already seen

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:22.083
up on the screen,

00:59:23.060 --> 00:59:25.560
human characters, natural elements

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:27.333
give them cover and protection,

00:59:27.333 --> 00:59:30.210
while also simultaneously,

00:59:30.210 --> 00:59:33.230
reaching out to draw
them into the darkness.

00:59:33.230 --> 00:59:35.630
This darkness is a retreat and a cover,

00:59:35.630 --> 00:59:37.660
but it also has an ominous and creeping,

00:59:37.660 --> 00:59:39.283
and inescapable quality.

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:41.890
They sanitize the topic,

00:59:41.890 --> 00:59:44.540
that Walpole admitted was disgusting.

00:59:44.540 --> 00:59:46.100
He adored Beauclerk's drawings,

00:59:46.100 --> 00:59:48.370
because he said they're expressive,

00:59:48.370 --> 00:59:51.457
but quote, they do not
shock like the original.

00:59:51.457 --> 00:59:52.660
Unquote.

00:59:52.660 --> 00:59:53.900
The drawings have this ability

00:59:53.900 --> 00:59:55.350
to express the interplay

00:59:55.350 --> 00:59:58.590
between reason, nature,
penitence and superstition,

00:59:58.590 --> 01:00:01.026
in a way that separates
them from the incest plot

01:00:01.026 --> 01:00:04.200
that propels those themes in the play.

01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:05.770
They're emblems of his ideas,

01:00:05.770 --> 01:00:07.830
and in a way they're a guide

01:00:07.830 --> 01:00:11.210
to reading "The Mysterious Mother" right.

01:00:11.210 --> 01:00:12.043
Thank you.

01:00:13.008 --> 01:00:16.008
(audience applause)

01:00:17.380 --> 01:00:19.283
- Thank you so much Nicole.

01:00:20.599 --> 01:00:23.940
Our next speaker is Cheryl Nixon.

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:25.420
She is an Associate Provost

01:00:25.420 --> 01:00:27.980
at the University of
Massachusetts, Boston.

01:00:27.980 --> 01:00:31.040
She also serves as an
Associate Professor of English

01:00:31.040 --> 01:00:33.170
specializing in 18th century literature.

01:00:33.170 --> 01:00:36.006
The rise of the novel
in law and literature.

01:00:36.006 --> 01:00:38.620
She is the author of "The Orphan

01:00:40.301 --> 01:00:42.112
in Eighteenth-century Law and Literature:

01:00:42.112 --> 01:00:45.130
"Estate, Blood and Body,"
published by AshGate in 2011.

01:00:47.250 --> 01:00:48.900
In addition to editing an anthology

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:51.850
of commentaries on the
novel, "Novel Definitions,"

01:00:51.850 --> 01:00:53.360
she's published articles on topics,

01:00:53.360 --> 01:00:56.920
such as guardianship
and legal recordkeeping,

01:00:56.920 --> 01:01:01.070
and I was inspired by, this glimpse

01:01:01.070 --> 01:01:05.070
into her activities as a scholar,

01:01:05.070 --> 01:01:06.760
and teacher and citizen.

01:01:06.760 --> 01:01:08.140
She comments.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:10.790
Cheryl comments that her
favorite form of scholarship

01:01:11.873 --> 01:01:14.070
has been creating public
rare book exhibitions

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:16.682
with her students for
the Boston Public Library

01:01:16.682 --> 01:01:20.880
including the exhibit "Crooks, Rogues,

01:01:20.880 --> 01:01:23.160
and Maids Less Than Virtuous."

01:01:23.160 --> 01:01:27.660
"Books in the streets
of 18th century London,"

01:01:27.660 --> 01:01:30.240
and her talk today is about,

01:01:30.240 --> 01:01:32.300
not the Mysterious Mother,

01:01:32.300 --> 01:01:35.103
but "The mysterious orphan" in this play.

01:01:41.082 --> 01:01:42.710
- Great, thank you.

01:01:42.710 --> 01:01:44.380
I think you can tell what the emphasis

01:01:44.380 --> 01:01:47.170
on the idea of the
orphan in my scholarship,

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:49.783
but indeed I will emphasize
the idea of the orphan.

01:01:50.620 --> 01:01:53.143
I guess my topic is not that mysterious.

01:01:54.580 --> 01:01:57.240
In this mini paper, I'd like to turn us

01:01:57.240 --> 01:01:59.550
to a reading of "The Mysterious Mother

01:01:59.550 --> 01:02:03.010
that emphasizes the
perspective of the child.

01:02:03.010 --> 01:02:04.910
I'd like to argue that the play

01:02:04.910 --> 01:02:07.605
dramatizes the family's ability

01:02:07.605 --> 01:02:10.060
to recreate the child,

01:02:10.060 --> 01:02:11.550
and more specifically,

01:02:11.550 --> 01:02:14.530
the parent's ability to redefine

01:02:14.530 --> 01:02:17.830
the biological child as an orphan.

01:02:17.830 --> 01:02:21.210
Obviously, I'm emphasizing
the Countess's positioning

01:02:21.210 --> 01:02:24.650
of her child Adeliza as an orphan,

01:02:24.650 --> 01:02:26.650
but I would also like to emphasize

01:02:26.650 --> 01:02:29.910
her positioning of her
son Edmund as an exile.

01:02:29.910 --> 01:02:34.030
a symbolic orphan barred
from his home and mother.

01:02:34.030 --> 01:02:37.064
Resulting from this
redefinition of the family,

01:02:37.064 --> 01:02:39.528
the play dramatizes the orphan's

01:02:39.528 --> 01:02:42.680
feelings of loss and confusion.

01:02:42.680 --> 01:02:44.630
Feelings of powerlessness.

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:45.900
Placelessness.

01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:47.840
Selflessness.

01:02:47.840 --> 01:02:51.370
Simply put, the mother's
purposeful transformation

01:02:51.370 --> 01:02:53.960
of her child into an orphan

01:02:57.412 --> 01:02:59.160
is an obvious act of familial betrayal,

01:02:59.160 --> 01:03:01.329
and I would like us to see orphaning

01:03:01.329 --> 01:03:04.710
as a shocking act of betrayal.

01:03:04.710 --> 01:03:07.120
Although of course we focus on incest

01:03:07.120 --> 01:03:09.913
as the play's most
horrible act of betrayal,

01:03:11.948 --> 01:03:13.470
I'm going to argue that orphaning

01:03:13.470 --> 01:03:15.020
is just as striking and shocking.

01:03:15.020 --> 01:03:18.140
Let me convince you that orphaning

01:03:20.187 --> 01:03:22.210
is one of Walpole's key
concerns in the play.

01:03:22.210 --> 01:03:24.030
I'll emphasize three points.

01:03:24.030 --> 01:03:26.410
That the orphan is a duplicated figure,

01:03:26.410 --> 01:03:27.840
throughout the play.

01:03:27.840 --> 01:03:31.020
Therefore it offers structure to the play.

01:03:31.020 --> 01:03:35.300
That orphaning is a strategy
of familial reconfiguration

01:03:35.300 --> 01:03:39.343
that furthers incest's
familial redefinition.

01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:42.763
Orphaning is kind of on
the spectrum with incest,

01:03:44.702 --> 01:03:47.120
and orphaning emphasizes the
activity of interpretation

01:03:47.120 --> 01:03:50.620
and the idea of unequal
knowledge, unequal power,

01:03:50.620 --> 01:03:52.563
in that act of interpretation.

01:03:54.500 --> 01:03:55.840
Obviously there's a duplication

01:03:55.840 --> 01:03:57.990
of orphan figures throughout the play.

01:03:57.990 --> 01:04:01.090
So that we do not miss
the idea of the orphan.

01:04:01.090 --> 01:04:02.573
I'll make the obvious point,

01:04:03.644 --> 01:04:04.477
that the orphan is repeated

01:04:05.369 --> 01:04:07.110
and becomes a way of
structuring character,

01:04:07.110 --> 01:04:08.110
emphasizing the children's dependent

01:04:08.110 --> 01:04:10.080
relationship on the Countess.

01:04:10.080 --> 01:04:11.760
Although we are used to emphasizing

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:13.456
the Countess's emotions,

01:04:13.456 --> 01:04:16.830
the play obviously emphasizes
the emotion of the children

01:04:16.830 --> 01:04:18.560
Adeliza and Edmund.

01:04:18.560 --> 01:04:20.480
The pain of homelessness.

01:04:20.480 --> 01:04:22.420
The pain of not knowing one's parents.

01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:24.513
Of being rejected, of being exiled.

01:04:25.469 --> 01:04:30.469
With Adaliza, the orphan
ward overseen by the Countess

01:04:30.470 --> 01:04:32.580
is obviously our key figure here,

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:33.990
and there's many many quotes I could read,

01:04:33.990 --> 01:04:35.650
but I'm just going to pick a few key quotes

01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:37.681
for each character.

01:04:37.681 --> 01:04:41.280
Adaliza emphasizes the
mystery of family origins

01:04:41.280 --> 01:04:42.320
that she represents,

01:04:42.320 --> 01:04:44.650
and she's constantly questioning

01:04:44.650 --> 01:04:45.740
who are my parents?

01:04:45.740 --> 01:04:46.893
Where am I coming from?

01:04:47.836 --> 01:04:49.651
Here's just one example of that,

01:04:49.651 --> 01:04:50.484
that occurs early in the play.

01:04:51.523 --> 01:04:54.520
The Countess says, "thou dearest
orphan to my bossom come,

01:04:54.520 --> 01:04:57.170
and vent thy little sorrows,"

01:04:57.170 --> 01:05:00.790
and Adeliza continues,
then ultimately says,

01:05:00.790 --> 01:05:01.930
"when have I wept,

01:05:01.930 --> 01:05:04.050
but when thou hast refused

01:05:04.050 --> 01:05:07.250
to let that Adeliza call thee mother.

01:05:07.250 --> 01:05:09.640
I know I was not worthy of such an honor.

01:05:09.640 --> 01:05:12.057
Too splendid for a child of charity.

01:05:12.057 --> 01:05:14.370
I know I am most unworthy."

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:19.370
And of course, we know that
Adeliza is verging on truth

01:05:19.930 --> 01:05:21.620
by wanting to call her the mother,

01:05:21.620 --> 01:05:24.420
but that kind of confusion and weeping

01:05:24.420 --> 01:05:25.790
and saying I am not unworthy,

01:05:25.790 --> 01:05:27.600
but I want to call you my mother.

01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:29.090
That confusion of feelings,

01:05:29.090 --> 01:05:32.223
is I think what Walpole's
emphasizing here.

01:05:33.350 --> 01:05:36.670
I'll move on to Edmund as an exiled child.

01:05:36.670 --> 01:05:38.050
I'd like to also argue that

01:05:38.050 --> 01:05:41.198
he plays a bit of a
role of an orphan also.

01:05:41.198 --> 01:05:44.220
My research is on the legal orphan,

01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:48.452
and legally a child who
is inheriting an estate,

01:05:48.452 --> 01:05:50.560
if the father dies,

01:05:50.560 --> 01:05:52.617
they would have legally
been considered an orphan

01:05:52.617 --> 01:05:55.760
unless the mother had
been specifically named

01:05:55.760 --> 01:05:58.010
as the guardian of the child.

01:05:58.010 --> 01:06:02.210
I think that probably would
have been the case in this story

01:06:02.210 --> 01:06:04.900
because of the way, as
we were just hearing,

01:06:04.900 --> 01:06:08.620
the Countess oversees
the estates of Edmund.

01:06:08.620 --> 01:06:10.712
However, I think we want to emphasize that

01:06:10.712 --> 01:06:12.750
it would have been very common

01:06:12.750 --> 01:06:15.090
for a child that lost a father

01:06:15.090 --> 01:06:17.080
to have been put in the category of orphan

01:06:17.080 --> 01:06:19.000
in the 18th century.

01:06:19.000 --> 01:06:20.460
When we first meet Edmund,

01:06:20.460 --> 01:06:22.453
he emphasized this banishment,

01:06:25.421 --> 01:06:27.024
this rejection by his mother.

01:06:27.024 --> 01:06:27.983
He sort of is duly deparented.

01:06:29.486 --> 01:06:31.080
This is of course the obvious
thing you saw last night,

01:06:31.080 --> 01:06:34.540
where he describes stealing
into Beatrice's chamber

01:06:34.540 --> 01:06:35.890
and that's where again we know

01:06:35.890 --> 01:06:39.280
that it's verging on the
understanding of incest

01:06:39.280 --> 01:06:41.380
that he's not quite seeing.

01:06:41.380 --> 01:06:44.870
Quote, "How my mother
became apprised, I know not,

01:06:44.870 --> 01:06:47.430
but her heart never too partial to me,

01:06:47.430 --> 01:06:49.250
grew estranged.

01:06:49.250 --> 01:06:52.780
Estranged, aversion in its fellest mood

01:06:52.780 --> 01:06:54.250
scowled from her eye,

01:06:54.250 --> 01:06:56.300
and drove me from her sight.

01:06:56.300 --> 01:06:58.140
She called me impious.

01:06:58.140 --> 01:06:59.620
Named my honest lewdness

01:06:59.620 --> 01:07:02.300
a profanation of my father's ashes.

01:07:02.300 --> 01:07:05.770
I knelt and wept." And it continues on

01:07:05.770 --> 01:07:07.860
talking about bathing her hand with tears,

01:07:07.860 --> 01:07:08.980
and how she snatches it away,

01:07:08.980 --> 01:07:10.270
and how it's horrible.

01:07:10.270 --> 01:07:12.730
Very overwraught emotions.

01:07:12.730 --> 01:07:14.310
Later Florian asks,

01:07:14.310 --> 01:07:15.480
why would you return?

01:07:15.480 --> 01:07:16.800
Why do you return to this?

01:07:16.800 --> 01:07:18.850
Why do you want to return home?

01:07:18.850 --> 01:07:21.290
Edmund says I am weary Florian

01:07:21.290 --> 01:07:22.980
of such a vagrant life.

01:07:22.980 --> 01:07:25.640
We heard that line last night also.

01:07:25.640 --> 01:07:30.640
That sense of feeling
estranged, rejected, weary.

01:07:31.428 --> 01:07:32.990
Excessive emotion.

01:07:32.990 --> 01:07:35.123
Edmund's constantly referring to that.

01:07:36.650 --> 01:07:40.390
I'd like to emphasize as another example

01:07:40.390 --> 01:07:42.070
of the repetition of orphans,

01:07:42.070 --> 01:07:44.070
the chorus of orphans,

01:07:44.070 --> 01:07:45.810
that we heard briefly last night

01:07:45.810 --> 01:07:47.700
and sort of had a shadowy figure

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:49.491
of some figures up on the screen

01:07:49.491 --> 01:07:51.830
representing the orphans.

01:07:51.830 --> 01:07:54.910
Obviously here I think Walpole

01:07:54.910 --> 01:07:57.160
is doing a very over the top duplication

01:07:57.160 --> 01:07:58.460
of the idea of the orphan,

01:07:59.668 --> 01:08:00.501
so we do not miss.

01:08:00.501 --> 01:08:02.453
We do not miss the figure of the orphan.

01:08:03.550 --> 01:08:06.050
They witness supernatural evidence

01:08:06.050 --> 01:08:07.980
of the family's sin,

01:08:07.980 --> 01:08:10.103
and they importantly do not understand it.

01:08:10.103 --> 01:08:12.870
This would be a point that

01:08:12.870 --> 01:08:15.400
I'll emphasize a little
bit later in the paper.

01:08:15.400 --> 01:08:18.470
The Countess when she first
introduces the orphans

01:08:18.470 --> 01:08:20.510
calls them my little orphans.

01:08:20.510 --> 01:08:23.100
These are part of as a benefactress

01:08:23.100 --> 01:08:25.580
who is benefiting from her charity.

01:08:25.580 --> 01:08:27.090
The orphans sing a hymn.

01:08:27.090 --> 01:08:29.950
I'll just read the last
two stanzas of the hymn,

01:08:29.950 --> 01:08:31.270
but I want to emphasize here,

01:08:31.270 --> 01:08:33.966
is that they are playing the role,

01:08:33.966 --> 01:08:38.966
of facing or covering up
the family crime also.

01:08:39.020 --> 01:08:40.190
The orphan's hymn goes,

01:08:40.190 --> 01:08:43.125
hear us harmless orphans hear,

01:08:43.125 --> 01:08:47.770
for who who dries our falling tear.

01:08:47.770 --> 01:08:51.130
Hush her sorrows, calm her breast.

01:08:51.130 --> 01:08:54.640
Give her what she gives us, rest.

01:08:54.640 --> 01:08:58.170
Guard our spotless souls from sin.

01:08:58.170 --> 01:09:01.120
Grant us virtues palm to win.

01:09:01.120 --> 01:09:03.870
Clothe the penitent with grace,

01:09:03.870 --> 01:09:08.870
and guilt's foul spots efface, efface."

01:09:09.320 --> 01:09:11.150
Here obviously they're
positioning themselves

01:09:11.150 --> 01:09:13.180
in relationship to the Countess.

01:09:13.180 --> 01:09:14.610
They're not her children,

01:09:14.610 --> 01:09:16.528
but in a way they are her children.

01:09:16.528 --> 01:09:18.184
They're her little orphans,

01:09:18.184 --> 01:09:20.200
that are trying to come up with

01:09:20.200 --> 01:09:22.854
a way to calm her breast,

01:09:22.854 --> 01:09:25.570
to hush her sorrows,

01:09:25.570 --> 01:09:28.770
and obviously they end with that line.

01:09:28.770 --> 01:09:32.000
They're trying to efface her guilt

01:09:34.293 --> 01:09:35.830
and the foul spots on her.

01:09:35.830 --> 01:09:38.590
The orphan becomes a cover or a mask

01:09:38.590 --> 01:09:39.923
for the source of guilt.

01:09:40.999 --> 01:09:43.240
We could continue to analyze

01:09:43.240 --> 01:09:44.940
each of these characters in greater depth,

01:09:44.940 --> 01:09:46.830
but what I want to emphasize

01:09:46.830 --> 01:09:49.640
is that the orphan is a larger structure,

01:09:49.640 --> 01:09:52.712
in structuring the obsessions of the play,

01:09:52.712 --> 01:09:55.300
and orphaning is a strategy.

01:09:55.300 --> 01:09:56.410
My second point is that orphaning

01:09:56.410 --> 01:09:59.386
is a strategy of familial reconfiguration,

01:09:59.386 --> 01:10:02.653
that furthers the incest theme.

01:10:04.080 --> 01:10:05.720
Orphaning provides another example

01:10:05.720 --> 01:10:09.260
of Walpole exploring the
limits of family bonds

01:10:09.260 --> 01:10:14.120
and asking, what is the
breaking point of the family?

01:10:14.120 --> 01:10:15.510
The Countess's activity,

01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:18.320
of redefining the child as an orphan,

01:10:18.320 --> 01:10:21.853
parallels or compounds
the crime of incest.

01:10:21.853 --> 01:10:23.100
As we were just hearing,

01:10:23.100 --> 01:10:24.790
I think we're very comfortable asking,

01:10:24.790 --> 01:10:26.884
what's the source of the Countess's pain,

01:10:26.884 --> 01:10:29.859
and emphasizing that
hidden idea of incest,

01:10:29.859 --> 01:10:33.610
and I think that we also
want to emphasize this

01:10:33.610 --> 01:10:36.290
questioning that is
coming from the children.

01:10:36.290 --> 01:10:38.420
Why did she banish Edmund,

01:10:38.420 --> 01:10:40.780
and who is Adeliza's parents?

01:10:40.780 --> 01:10:43.460
This incest question leads
to these further questions

01:10:43.460 --> 01:10:44.760
that involve the children.

01:10:45.621 --> 01:10:48.710
Orphaning provides, not
just another example,

01:10:48.710 --> 01:10:52.350
of her immoral manipulation of the family,

01:10:52.350 --> 01:10:55.930
but provides an example
of her conscious decision

01:10:55.930 --> 01:10:58.242
to extend that manipulation.

01:10:58.242 --> 01:11:00.675
To give it structure and permanence

01:11:00.675 --> 01:11:04.500
by making it an overt part of her family.

01:11:04.500 --> 01:11:06.400
The Countess believes that orphaning

01:11:06.400 --> 01:11:08.427
can hide her crime of incest.

01:11:08.427 --> 01:11:12.770
Can in effect, provide a
solution to the incest.

01:11:12.770 --> 01:11:15.660
However, we know this is twisted logic.

01:11:15.660 --> 01:11:17.650
I think as good readers we know,

01:11:17.650 --> 01:11:18.860
any solutions like this,

01:11:18.860 --> 01:11:20.810
is only going to compound

01:11:20.810 --> 01:11:23.030
and further that original crime

01:11:23.030 --> 01:11:24.913
and not succeed in masking it.

01:11:26.170 --> 01:11:27.970
What I would like us to consider,

01:11:27.970 --> 01:11:31.200
is that the crime of purposeful orphaning

01:11:31.200 --> 01:11:34.174
is in some ways even more horrific

01:11:34.174 --> 01:11:37.300
than incest for two reasons.

01:11:37.300 --> 01:11:40.573
First, because it's an
expression of excess.

01:11:40.573 --> 01:11:44.351
Excessive desire, of course,

01:11:44.351 --> 01:11:46.070
leads to the incest,

01:11:46.070 --> 01:11:48.620
but also leads to, and is accompanied by,

01:11:48.620 --> 01:11:51.506
this excessive rejection of the child.

01:11:51.506 --> 01:11:53.470
In some ways, these two opposites

01:11:53.470 --> 01:11:55.473
are yolked together through orphaning.

01:11:56.361 --> 01:11:59.113
Excessive desire, and excessive rejection.

01:11:59.113 --> 01:12:00.610
I want to emphasize
they're both excessive.

01:12:00.610 --> 01:12:02.100
They're both very over the top,

01:12:02.100 --> 01:12:04.516
because of this duplication of orphaning,

01:12:04.516 --> 01:12:09.210
and secondly because it's not
just a singular occurrence

01:12:09.210 --> 01:12:11.880
the orphaning becomes a daily occurrence.

01:12:11.880 --> 01:12:14.696
It's a form of family
betrayal that's repeated

01:12:14.696 --> 01:12:16.912
and what I want to emphasize is

01:12:16.912 --> 01:12:19.060
it becomes routinized, normalized.

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:22.097
It's a planned form of excess.

01:12:22.097 --> 01:12:25.570
Again, it's even in that group of orphans,

01:12:25.570 --> 01:12:29.540
that she positions an
entire chorus of orphans

01:12:29.540 --> 01:12:31.410
that is receiving her charity.

01:12:31.410 --> 01:12:32.890
It becomes normalized,

01:12:32.890 --> 01:12:34.500
and that's the horror,

01:12:34.500 --> 01:12:35.840
is that it's become daily.

01:12:35.840 --> 01:12:37.663
It's become routine.

01:12:38.619 --> 01:12:39.943
It's planned excess.

01:12:42.155 --> 01:12:43.410
As my third point,

01:12:43.410 --> 01:12:46.100
I want to emphasize that orphaning,

01:12:46.100 --> 01:12:49.120
is connected to the
activity of interpretation.

01:12:49.120 --> 01:12:51.970
The orphan introduces the
unknown into the family.

01:12:51.970 --> 01:12:53.260
Who are your parents?

01:12:53.260 --> 01:12:54.720
Who are your siblings?

01:12:54.720 --> 01:12:55.890
Where did you come from?

01:12:55.890 --> 01:12:57.280
Where are you going?

01:12:57.280 --> 01:12:59.910
The orphan is a mystery
that needs to be solved,

01:12:59.910 --> 01:13:02.310
and these characters are
constantly questioning,

01:13:02.310 --> 01:13:05.500
who am I because of their orphan status.

01:13:05.500 --> 01:13:07.160
The orphan thus heightens

01:13:07.160 --> 01:13:09.015
our interest in interpreting
the family as readers.

01:13:09.015 --> 01:13:12.570
We want to determine, what
is its true structure?

01:13:12.570 --> 01:13:13.970
What is its true meaning?

01:13:13.970 --> 01:13:16.010
The orphaning keeps teasing us,

01:13:16.010 --> 01:13:18.833
to try to interpret what is
at the heart of this family?

01:13:19.700 --> 01:13:21.500
I would like to emphasize again,

01:13:21.500 --> 01:13:23.750
a seemingly unimportant
element of the play.

01:13:23.750 --> 01:13:25.540
This chorus of orphans.

01:13:25.540 --> 01:13:28.637
These orphans emphasize
the act of interpretation

01:13:28.637 --> 01:13:32.450
because they witness the
supernatural destruction

01:13:32.450 --> 01:13:34.927
of the monument to the Count of Narbonne.

01:13:34.927 --> 01:13:37.800
They ask us to question
what does this mean?

01:13:37.800 --> 01:13:40.760
What does this supernatural moment mean?

01:13:40.760 --> 01:13:44.526
I'll just read a brief
excerpt from that scene.

01:13:44.526 --> 01:13:46.670
The orphans are going in,

01:13:46.670 --> 01:13:50.620
to see the Count of Narbonne's monument

01:13:50.620 --> 01:13:53.577
and first orphan says,
"oh a storm so dreadful.

01:13:53.577 --> 01:13:56.760
Some demon rides the air."

01:13:56.760 --> 01:13:58.593
He continues describing the storm.

01:13:59.656 --> 01:14:02.161
Martin says, "Twas well, but none of you,"

01:14:02.161 --> 01:14:02.994
meaning the orphans,

01:14:02.994 --> 01:14:04.320
"none of you had the courage

01:14:04.320 --> 01:14:07.260
to face the fiend, to face the specter."

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:08.700
They continue a bit back and forth,

01:14:08.700 --> 01:14:11.620
and the second orphan says, "I winked.

01:14:11.620 --> 01:14:14.800
I saw the lightning
burst on the monument.

01:14:14.800 --> 01:14:18.200
The shield of arms shivered to splinters.

01:14:18.200 --> 01:14:21.010
ere I could repeat an Ave Maria.

01:14:21.010 --> 01:14:23.020
Down came the hideous crash.

01:14:23.020 --> 01:14:26.203
The cross came tumbling, and then I fled."

01:14:27.050 --> 01:14:28.660
Martin says "retire.

01:14:28.660 --> 01:14:30.740
This is unholy ground."

01:14:30.740 --> 01:14:34.070
Last night we had that image of 

01:14:34.070 --> 01:14:35.490
I really liked that there was that image

01:14:35.490 --> 01:14:39.564
of the broken monument
hovering over most of the play.

01:14:39.564 --> 01:14:44.248
That's symbolic
representation of the father,

01:14:44.248 --> 01:14:47.812
and his response to what's
happening in the family.

01:14:47.812 --> 01:14:49.790
What is this broken monument?

01:14:49.790 --> 01:14:51.550
Why is it broken?

01:14:51.550 --> 01:14:53.843
I think it's important that the orphans,

01:14:54.772 --> 01:14:55.800
this group of orphans are pointing

01:14:55.800 --> 01:14:56.883
to that monument.

01:14:57.960 --> 01:15:00.230
Thus orphaning emphasizes

01:15:00.230 --> 01:15:02.630
a lack of knowledge about the family,

01:15:02.630 --> 01:15:05.440
and it also emphasizes unequal knowledge

01:15:05.440 --> 01:15:06.720
and unequal power,

01:15:06.720 --> 01:15:08.974
in that act of interpretation.

01:15:08.974 --> 01:15:11.600
Orphaning raises questions
that need to be answered,

01:15:11.600 --> 01:15:13.840
but the answers are unclear.

01:15:13.840 --> 01:15:14.830
Of course here we know,

01:15:14.830 --> 01:15:17.423
it's only the Countess
that has the true answers.

01:15:18.260 --> 01:15:20.300
Orphaning raises
confusion about parentage.

01:15:20.300 --> 01:15:21.460
Confusion about place.

01:15:21.460 --> 01:15:24.913
Confusion about the role in the family.

01:15:25.761 --> 01:15:30.140
When Edmund is seeing his
mother for the first time,

01:15:30.140 --> 01:15:32.410
we know, and I'll just list
a couple of key quotes.

01:15:32.410 --> 01:15:34.140
The Countess responds,

01:15:34.140 --> 01:15:35.573
and we saw this again, stage last night,

01:15:35.573 --> 01:15:39.100
that Countess says ha is this sorcery,

01:15:39.100 --> 01:15:41.130
or is it my husband?"

01:15:41.130 --> 01:15:44.660
Edmund replies, "your parent, mother."

01:15:44.660 --> 01:15:47.860
Correcting her, saying I am your son.

01:15:47.860 --> 01:15:49.425
You are my mother.

01:15:49.425 --> 01:15:51.740
The lack of knowledge about the family

01:15:51.740 --> 01:15:55.087
leads to confusion, leads to displacement,

01:15:55.087 --> 01:15:57.963
of the positions of the family.

01:15:58.850 --> 01:16:01.217
Another example of Adeliza says,

01:16:01.217 --> 01:16:05.460
"alas when I was then both a child of sin,

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:07.310
who were my parents?

01:16:07.310 --> 01:16:09.100
I must pray for them."

01:16:09.100 --> 01:16:11.230
But she does not know who her parents are,

01:16:11.230 --> 01:16:13.300
and the Countess obviously
will not tell her.

01:16:13.300 --> 01:16:15.381
A key moment of course,

01:16:15.381 --> 01:16:19.431
is the horror of learning about parentage.

01:16:19.431 --> 01:16:21.770
When that question of
orphaning gets answered,

01:16:21.770 --> 01:16:25.343
it also answers the question with incest.

01:16:26.420 --> 01:16:28.820
I'll just read a little
bit from that key scene,

01:16:29.690 --> 01:16:34.470
when Edmund is confronting
his mother with his marriage,

01:16:34.470 --> 01:16:37.470
and Edmund says, "Dear Parent, look on us,

01:16:37.470 --> 01:16:39.720
and bless your children."

01:16:39.720 --> 01:16:42.620
And the Countess replies, "my children.

01:16:42.620 --> 01:16:44.330
Horror, horror.

01:16:44.330 --> 01:16:45.830
Yes too sure.

01:16:45.830 --> 01:16:47.210
Ye are my children."

01:16:47.210 --> 01:16:50.550
That claiming that these are not orphans

01:16:50.550 --> 01:16:51.820
but these are her children.

01:16:51.820 --> 01:16:54.330
She's doing that with this full knowledge

01:16:54.330 --> 01:16:55.600
that involves incest,

01:16:55.600 --> 01:16:57.130
which of course at this moment,

01:16:57.130 --> 01:17:00.100
Edmund doesn't understand
what that claiming means.

01:17:00.100 --> 01:17:03.100
She continues, Edmund loose that hand.

01:17:03.100 --> 01:17:05.060
It is poison to thy soul.

01:17:05.060 --> 01:17:08.160
Hell has no venom like a child's touch.

01:17:08.160 --> 01:17:09.720
Oh, agonizing thought.

01:17:09.720 --> 01:17:11.610
Who made this marriage?

01:17:11.610 --> 01:17:16.610
Whose unhollowed breath
pronounced the incestuous sounds?"

01:17:16.980 --> 01:17:19.080
That's where we get that word incest,

01:17:19.080 --> 01:17:21.070
but I think I want us to emphasize

01:17:21.070 --> 01:17:23.270
that before we get to
the moment of incest,

01:17:23.270 --> 01:17:25.520
we get to the claiming of the child.

01:17:25.520 --> 01:17:28.990
We get to the question
of resolving orphaning.

01:17:28.990 --> 01:17:30.940
We get this unique repetition

01:17:30.940 --> 01:17:33.240
of the naming of familial position,

01:17:33.240 --> 01:17:35.500
throughout this entire scene.

01:17:35.500 --> 01:17:37.820
Let me read a little
bit more of the scene,

01:17:37.820 --> 01:17:40.270
and again, emphasizing
that there has to be

01:17:40.270 --> 01:17:43.810
this naming and renaming
of familial position,

01:17:43.810 --> 01:17:45.370
because people, the characters,

01:17:45.370 --> 01:17:47.750
are trying to interpret what is happening,

01:17:47.750 --> 01:17:50.140
and there's this unequal knowledge,

01:17:50.140 --> 01:17:51.700
where the Countess understands

01:17:51.700 --> 01:17:53.290
the true familiar positions,

01:17:53.290 --> 01:17:55.275
but the children do not.

01:17:55.275 --> 01:18:00.263
The Countess continues, "lo'
where this mother stands.

01:18:01.684 --> 01:18:05.150
Excuse me, where this monster stands.

01:18:05.150 --> 01:18:07.510
Thy mother, mistress.

01:18:07.510 --> 01:18:09.610
The mother of thy daughter.

01:18:09.610 --> 01:18:11.910
Sister, wife.

01:18:11.910 --> 01:18:15.420
The pillar of accumulated horrors."

01:18:15.420 --> 01:18:17.940
She has to run through
all of these positions

01:18:17.940 --> 01:18:19.260
within the family.

01:18:19.260 --> 01:18:20.960
She's a monster because she's a mother,

01:18:20.960 --> 01:18:22.752
mistress, daughter, sister, wife.

01:18:22.752 --> 01:18:25.350
All of this collapsed together,

01:18:25.350 --> 01:18:27.140
and trying to figure out
the correct structuring

01:18:27.140 --> 01:18:29.780
of the family, and again, I would argue,

01:18:29.780 --> 01:18:30.810
that it's not just incest,

01:18:30.810 --> 01:18:32.720
but it's orphaning that has created

01:18:32.720 --> 01:18:34.253
this familial confusion.

01:18:36.210 --> 01:18:38.130
To conclude as I hope I've shown,

01:18:38.130 --> 01:18:39.000
the orphan is central

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:42.410
to determining the logic
of Walpole's horror.

01:18:42.410 --> 01:18:45.150
It's central to the
structuring of the play.

01:18:45.150 --> 01:18:48.390
This is obviously a play
about familial horror,

01:18:48.390 --> 01:18:50.820
about familial betrayal.

01:18:50.820 --> 01:18:53.108
This horror comes from the ability

01:18:53.108 --> 01:18:57.320
to redefine and restructure the family.

01:18:57.320 --> 01:19:00.380
But I would argue it's a
restructuring that occurs

01:19:00.380 --> 01:19:02.580
not just through incest,

01:19:02.580 --> 01:19:04.520
but through orphaning.

01:19:04.520 --> 01:19:05.575
Thank you.

01:19:05.575 --> 01:19:08.575
(audience applause)

01:19:18.919 --> 01:19:20.487
- Thank you Cheryl.

01:19:20.487 --> 01:19:23.680
Our last speaker is Nicole Wright.

01:19:23.680 --> 01:19:25.650
Nicole is an Assistant
Professor of English

01:19:25.650 --> 01:19:28.394
at the University of Colorado, at Boulder.

01:19:28.394 --> 01:19:31.810
She was recently a
Lapidis long term fellow

01:19:31.810 --> 01:19:33.770
at the Shoenberg Center

01:19:33.770 --> 01:19:35.670
at the New York Public Library.

01:19:35.670 --> 01:19:38.520
Her articles have appeared
in "Eighteenth Century Fiction,"

01:19:38.520 --> 01:19:40.170
Eighteenth Century Studies,"

01:19:40.170 --> 01:19:42.350
"Eighteenth Century Theory
and Interpretation,"

01:19:42.350 --> 01:19:43.730
"The Toronto Quarterly,"

01:19:43.730 --> 01:19:46.048
and "The Chronicle of Higher Education."

01:19:46.048 --> 01:19:51.048
She will speak today
to us on "'Kindest Laws':

01:19:51.370 --> 01:19:54.423
Intimate Adjudication in
'The Mysterious Mother'."

01:20:02.550 --> 01:20:04.670
- Thank you Jill for the introduction,

01:20:04.670 --> 01:20:09.180
and thank you to Cindy,
Nicole, Michelle, and Sue,

01:20:09.180 --> 01:20:11.865
for all they did to
prepare for this event,

01:20:11.865 --> 01:20:14.380
and to The Lewis Walpole Library

01:20:14.380 --> 01:20:16.780
for fostering some of my earliest

01:20:16.780 --> 01:20:20.550
navigations of the archive
and its serendipities.

01:20:20.550 --> 01:20:23.583
That term that Walpole popularized.

01:20:25.320 --> 01:20:28.590
"What awful silence," Florian exclaims

01:20:28.590 --> 01:20:31.110
at the beginning of "The Mysterious Mother."

01:20:31.110 --> 01:20:33.490
With this sentence, the very first act,

01:20:33.490 --> 01:20:36.640
the very first line of the first act,

01:20:36.640 --> 01:20:39.430
Florian breaks the prevailing quiet.

01:20:39.430 --> 01:20:42.110
Even as he directs attention to it.

01:20:42.110 --> 01:20:45.550
The descriptor awful
compounds the suggestion

01:20:45.550 --> 01:20:49.060
the holding intention
of opposing meanings.

01:20:49.060 --> 01:20:52.410
Simultaneously, connoting
the awe and reverence

01:20:52.410 --> 01:20:55.020
with which the faithful approach God,

01:20:55.020 --> 01:20:58.590
and the dread evoked by the untold tale,

01:20:58.590 --> 01:21:02.120
prior to the Countess's
ghastly revelation.

01:21:02.120 --> 01:21:04.420
From the very beginning then,

01:21:04.420 --> 01:21:06.670
Walpole's tragedy primes its audience

01:21:06.670 --> 01:21:11.200
to balance dueling sensory
conditions and affective states.

01:21:11.200 --> 01:21:13.522
Silence and cacophony, piety, and disgust.

01:21:13.522 --> 01:21:18.199
Disgusting is one of
the terms that Walpole

01:21:18.199 --> 01:21:23.199
notably uses when discussing
his play in correspondence.

01:21:23.560 --> 01:21:26.360
The Janus-faced phrasing
of the first line

01:21:26.360 --> 01:21:29.470
proceeds an abundance of similar locutions

01:21:29.470 --> 01:21:30.960
throughout the play.

01:21:30.960 --> 01:21:34.320
Such moments resonate as cues to the play's

01:21:34.320 --> 01:21:35.910
audience of readers,

01:21:35.910 --> 01:21:38.750
bringing to mind at
once original integrity,

01:21:38.750 --> 01:21:41.310
and postlapsarian brokenness.

01:21:41.310 --> 01:21:44.770
Essential honor and innate depravity.

01:21:44.770 --> 01:21:47.370
As the play continues
it thus becomes clear,

01:21:47.370 --> 01:21:51.300
that its dialogue reflects
a model for moral judgment.

01:21:51.300 --> 01:21:54.960
Establishing its readers
as alternative arbiters,

01:21:54.960 --> 01:21:58.088
who can evaluate the
Countess's crime of incest,

01:21:58.088 --> 01:22:00.810
with more perspecuity than allowed

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:02.560
by the villain of the piece.

01:22:02.560 --> 01:22:05.627
Friar Benedict, the predatory confessor.

01:22:05.627 --> 01:22:08.920
The Countess is striving for atonement.

01:22:08.920 --> 01:22:10.790
More specifically, her struggle

01:22:10.790 --> 01:22:14.070
to take back or compensate for her crime,

01:22:14.070 --> 01:22:16.830
has a formal analog I argue,

01:22:16.830 --> 01:22:19.762
in the terminology of
simultaneous reiteration

01:22:19.762 --> 01:22:23.210
and erasure that pervades the play.

01:22:23.210 --> 01:22:25.670
As the Countess alternately hints at,

01:22:25.670 --> 01:22:28.925
discloses, and renounces
her wrongful deed.

01:22:28.925 --> 01:22:32.640
Certainly the Countess can
be reviled as a nymphomaniac

01:22:32.640 --> 01:22:36.160
who abandons her duty to
mourn her venerable husband,

01:22:36.160 --> 01:22:38.080
on the very night his bloody corpse

01:22:38.080 --> 01:22:40.010
is delivered to her, no less,

01:22:40.010 --> 01:22:42.973
and instead satisfies her urges

01:22:42.973 --> 01:22:45.520
by deceiving her own
son into carnal embrace.

01:22:45.520 --> 01:22:47.530
Yet, her unceasing efforts to serve

01:22:47.530 --> 01:22:49.555
the most vulnerable
members of her community,

01:22:49.555 --> 01:22:52.270
and repent for her crime,

01:22:52.270 --> 01:22:55.400
are also foregrounded
via not only the plot,

01:22:55.400 --> 01:22:58.968
but also the diction and
syntax of the play's dialogue.

01:22:58.968 --> 01:23:01.440
The play's moral double vision

01:23:01.440 --> 01:23:04.270
is also sustained through
the Countess's habit,

01:23:04.270 --> 01:23:05.984
of palimpsest inscription,

01:23:05.984 --> 01:23:08.594
in which she composes messages

01:23:08.594 --> 01:23:13.540
that she subsequently
crosses out or weeps over

01:23:13.540 --> 01:23:16.400
to the point that they
are almost illegible,

01:23:16.400 --> 01:23:18.831
but can still be read in full

01:23:18.831 --> 01:23:21.181
by those who come into
possession of her works.

01:23:22.132 --> 01:23:23.820
As I will show, the Countess
and her children together

01:23:23.820 --> 01:23:26.000
produce not only a transgression

01:23:26.000 --> 01:23:28.650
that can be regretted
but never obliterated,

01:23:28.650 --> 01:23:32.140
but also a model for how lay
people ought to deliberate

01:23:32.140 --> 01:23:35.750
over even the most
apparently profound crimes,

01:23:35.750 --> 01:23:37.700
and note that the Countess prefers to use

01:23:37.700 --> 01:23:39.970
crime rather than sin.

01:23:39.970 --> 01:23:42.573
A distinction that is
significant for my argument.

01:23:43.770 --> 01:23:46.470
The model they demonstrate
abstains from judgment

01:23:46.470 --> 01:23:47.600
from a distance.

01:23:47.600 --> 01:23:49.810
From which characterological nuances

01:23:49.810 --> 01:23:51.250
cannot be perceived,

01:23:51.250 --> 01:23:54.510
and instead privileges
intimate adjudication

01:23:54.510 --> 01:23:56.160
that takes into consideration,

01:23:56.160 --> 01:23:57.988
Paimpsest moral complexity,

01:23:57.988 --> 01:24:01.985
to make up an account to
use the phrase favored

01:24:01.985 --> 01:24:06.020
by the Friars Martin and Benedict.

01:24:06.020 --> 01:24:09.940
I argue that the problem
confronted by the play's audience,

01:24:09.940 --> 01:24:13.880
how to judge the sensational
episode of double incest,

01:24:13.880 --> 01:24:15.947
is eliminated by the template of judgment

01:24:15.947 --> 01:24:18.410
that the Countess and Edmund generate

01:24:18.410 --> 01:24:21.770
for assessing those in a
variety of circumstances.

01:24:21.770 --> 01:24:23.310
Ranging from the poor souls

01:24:23.310 --> 01:24:26.370
who flock to share in the
Countess's benevolence,

01:24:26.370 --> 01:24:28.221
to the supposed infidels

01:24:28.221 --> 01:24:30.170
who fall to Edmund's sword,

01:24:30.170 --> 01:24:32.530
as they vie for power and sovereignty,

01:24:32.530 --> 01:24:34.783
with Christians in far off lands.

01:24:35.723 --> 01:24:38.800
Where else do we see
this model of judgment?

01:24:38.800 --> 01:24:41.147
This moral priming emerging on the page.

01:24:41.147 --> 01:24:45.240
One manifestation can be
found in the prefix un,

01:24:45.240 --> 01:24:47.070
which is repeatedly associated

01:24:47.070 --> 01:24:50.480
with the crime as she
terms it, of the Countess.

01:24:50.480 --> 01:24:52.940
"Quick, unsay the monstrous tale,"

01:24:52.940 --> 01:24:54.341
the Countess pleads upon hearing

01:24:54.341 --> 01:24:57.163
that her children Adeliza and Edmund

01:24:57.163 --> 01:25:00.280
have been united in matrimony by Benedict.

01:25:00.280 --> 01:25:04.455
The profusion of un terms,
intoned by the Countess,

01:25:04.455 --> 01:25:08.830
reflect her futile yearning
for her present words

01:25:08.830 --> 01:25:11.480
to nullify her previous actions.

01:25:11.480 --> 01:25:13.890
She asks Benedict whether he has come

01:25:13.890 --> 01:25:16.010
to tutor my unapt nature.

01:25:16.010 --> 01:25:17.673
Efface the stains.

01:25:18.580 --> 01:25:20.710
Or is her attempt really in vain?

01:25:20.710 --> 01:25:22.700
For evoking the impossibility

01:25:22.700 --> 01:25:25.130
of returning to her previous condition,

01:25:25.130 --> 01:25:27.070
of rectitude and dignity.

01:25:27.070 --> 01:25:29.110
Her pleas seemingly ineffectual

01:25:29.110 --> 01:25:30.453
in the world of the play,

01:25:31.393 --> 01:25:34.300
can conjure for the audience
of readers a double vision.

01:25:34.300 --> 01:25:36.040
Not only of her crime,

01:25:36.040 --> 01:25:39.173
but also her prior integrity
and current virtue.

01:25:40.208 --> 01:25:44.200
Thus cueing the audience to gauge

01:25:45.049 --> 01:25:48.470
how practicable it would
be to unsay the tale.

01:25:48.470 --> 01:25:50.060
Even if such a possibility,

01:25:50.060 --> 01:25:53.720
is dismissed nearly as
soon as it is raised.

01:25:53.720 --> 01:25:57.592
Referring to herself as the
unapt, unhinged, unnatural,

01:25:57.592 --> 01:26:00.575
perpetrator of this secret
sin, this untold tale,

01:26:00.575 --> 01:26:03.945
the Countess disassociates herself,

01:26:03.945 --> 01:26:08.360
from several desirable
honorable qualities,

01:26:08.360 --> 01:26:10.830
but the humiliated reason
with which she speaks

01:26:10.830 --> 01:26:14.950
of what Benedict calls
her unheard of crimes.

01:26:14.950 --> 01:26:17.730
Calls into question,
rather than reaffirms,

01:26:17.730 --> 01:26:21.050
the notion that she
cannot find redemption.

01:26:21.050 --> 01:26:25.550
After all other un reversals
are performed upon demand.

01:26:25.550 --> 01:26:29.320
As one Edmund ordered
Benedict, "unriddle priest.

01:26:29.320 --> 01:26:31.250
My soul is too impatient,

01:26:31.250 --> 01:26:34.330
to wait the impertinence
of flowery dialect."

01:26:34.330 --> 01:26:37.090
Or as when Florian later
commands the Friar,

01:26:37.090 --> 01:26:40.480
"by my good sword, thou
shall unriddle priest."

01:26:40.480 --> 01:26:42.470
The un injunctions are reminiscent

01:26:42.470 --> 01:26:46.230
of similar contemplations of
morally freighted reversal

01:26:46.230 --> 01:26:49.170
in Shakespeare whose literary influence

01:26:50.353 --> 01:26:52.160
on The Mysterious Mother
has long been noted.

01:26:52.160 --> 01:26:54.200
From Kent's reply in Leer,

01:26:54.200 --> 01:26:56.580
"I cannot wish the fall undone,"

01:26:56.580 --> 01:26:58.670
referring to the birth of his natural son.

01:26:58.670 --> 01:27:00.250
Also named Edmund.

01:27:00.250 --> 01:27:03.030
To Lady MacBeth's unsex me here,

01:27:03.030 --> 01:27:06.480
and fill me to the crown
from the crown to the toe,

01:27:06.480 --> 01:27:08.893
top-full. Of direst cruelty."

01:27:09.820 --> 01:27:11.390
and here you can see how this connects up

01:27:11.390 --> 01:27:14.403
with the rewriting of Macbeth
that Dale mentioned earlier.

01:27:15.330 --> 01:27:17.390
In the Shakespearean context,

01:27:17.390 --> 01:27:20.050
these wishes or demands
for moral reversals,

01:27:20.050 --> 01:27:22.970
may be repented of, retroactively denied,

01:27:22.970 --> 01:27:25.010
or incompletely fulfilled,

01:27:25.010 --> 01:27:26.430
but in each case the speaker's

01:27:26.430 --> 01:27:30.170
original morally blameless
state is brought to mind.

01:27:30.170 --> 01:27:33.040
Even if only through an uttered negation.

01:27:33.040 --> 01:27:36.193
Alongside their current moral condition.

01:27:37.030 --> 01:27:38.740
To further complicate matters,

01:27:38.740 --> 01:27:40.820
note that un is what linguists call

01:27:40.820 --> 01:27:42.760
a bound morphing.

01:27:42.760 --> 01:27:45.870
Bound because it does not
stand on its own as a word,

01:27:45.870 --> 01:27:49.000
and morphing for its
power to radically morph,

01:27:49.000 --> 01:27:52.120
change, reverse the word it modifies.

01:27:52.120 --> 01:27:54.173
The prefix un though is ambiguous

01:27:54.173 --> 01:27:56.510
in that while it sometimes

01:27:56.510 --> 01:27:59.270
connotes the reversal of
some original condition,

01:27:59.270 --> 01:28:01.590
it does not necessarily do so.

01:28:01.590 --> 01:28:04.393
It can either negate
an excellent condition,

01:28:04.393 --> 01:28:06.940
or indicate that a given condition

01:28:06.940 --> 01:28:09.450
never existed in the first place.

01:28:09.450 --> 01:28:11.230
While the guilt ridden Countess

01:28:11.230 --> 01:28:13.190
frequently uses un words,

01:28:13.190 --> 01:28:15.840
so does her blameless daughter Adeliza.

01:28:15.840 --> 01:28:18.840
For the Countess, un means reversal

01:28:18.840 --> 01:28:22.460
Adeliza un suggests an
original moral purity

01:28:22.460 --> 01:28:24.040
that remains intact,

01:28:24.040 --> 01:28:27.360
at least until she is wed
to her brother Edmund.

01:28:27.360 --> 01:28:29.539
Prior to her unnatural union,

01:28:29.539 --> 01:28:33.300
Adeliza is a maiden of
unblemished innocence.

01:28:33.300 --> 01:28:36.790
She is unknowing and unfriended.

01:28:36.790 --> 01:28:39.309
Linguistically linked
through terms of negation,

01:28:39.309 --> 01:28:42.000
the guilty mother and
the innocent daughter

01:28:42.000 --> 01:28:44.720
blur together as both are associated

01:28:44.720 --> 01:28:47.656
with original moral goodness and dignity.

01:28:47.656 --> 01:28:50.200
Even Benedict, who presents himself

01:28:50.200 --> 01:28:52.180
as the play's moral aribiter,

01:28:52.180 --> 01:28:56.530
fosters this bifurcated split
screen view of the countess.

01:28:56.530 --> 01:28:58.490
During one of their frequent arguments,

01:28:58.490 --> 01:29:00.290
he taunts her before announcing

01:29:00.290 --> 01:29:02.780
the false news of Edmund's death.

01:29:02.780 --> 01:29:05.170
"Learn if the mother's fortitude,

01:29:05.170 --> 01:29:07.783
can brave the woman's arrogance."

01:29:09.130 --> 01:29:11.140
Here Benedict divides the Countess

01:29:11.140 --> 01:29:13.160
into the proud self serving woman,

01:29:13.160 --> 01:29:16.270
and the long suffering sacrificial mother.

01:29:16.270 --> 01:29:19.110
Elsewhere however, Benedict
defines the Countess

01:29:19.110 --> 01:29:21.305
solely in terms of her crime.

01:29:21.305 --> 01:29:23.590
His methodology of judgment

01:29:23.590 --> 01:29:26.950
is presented as one that
the reader must reject.

01:29:26.950 --> 01:29:30.880
He judges through gossip,
deduction, and insinuation.

01:29:30.880 --> 01:29:32.760
Benedict steps in as an arbiter

01:29:32.760 --> 01:29:35.443
to take advantage of the failure of law.

01:29:35.443 --> 01:29:37.847
Law and "The Mysterious Mother"

01:29:37.847 --> 01:29:40.279
is invoked primarily as

01:29:40.279 --> 01:29:42.140
a disempowered neutered force.

01:29:42.140 --> 01:29:46.320
That which Florian refers
to as nature's kindest laws,

01:29:46.320 --> 01:29:47.971
a locution whose very tautology

01:29:47.971 --> 01:29:51.176
evokes the play's double incest

01:29:51.176 --> 01:29:54.890
has led the Countess and Edmund astray

01:29:54.890 --> 01:29:56.815
as they follow their instinctive desires

01:29:56.815 --> 01:30:00.180
to satisfy their sexual appetites.

01:30:00.180 --> 01:30:01.920
Edmund prides himself on the fact

01:30:01.920 --> 01:30:06.310
that he has not sapped my
countries laws, unquote,

01:30:06.310 --> 01:30:10.003
but these laws likewise fail
to protect him from Perdition.

01:30:11.560 --> 01:30:13.780
Benedict disavows any responsibility

01:30:13.780 --> 01:30:16.510
to follow the laws of nation or king.

01:30:16.510 --> 01:30:17.978
Of the church, he states,

01:30:17.978 --> 01:30:21.700
"no laws I know but her prosperity.

01:30:21.700 --> 01:30:25.050
No country, but her
boundless acquisitions."

01:30:25.050 --> 01:30:26.540
Despite his apparent piety,

01:30:26.540 --> 01:30:29.331
he is ruled not by
theological considerations,

01:30:29.331 --> 01:30:32.010
but instead by his avarice.

01:30:32.010 --> 01:30:34.440
It is indicative of the
Countess's reinstated

01:30:34.440 --> 01:30:38.020
moral capacity that
she resists his efforts

01:30:38.020 --> 01:30:40.950
to stand in judgment of
her as her confessor.

01:30:40.950 --> 01:30:43.763
As Robert Miles notes,
the play advocates for

01:30:43.763 --> 01:30:46.960
the sanctity of the individual conscience.

01:30:46.960 --> 01:30:50.160
A self assertiveness
against the invasive power

01:30:50.160 --> 01:30:52.023
of the patriarchal confession.

01:30:53.040 --> 01:30:55.840
Rejecting the modes of
judgment offered by the law,

01:30:55.840 --> 01:30:59.470
the play instead advocates
for intimate adjudication.

01:30:59.470 --> 01:31:04.020
A practice relying on
proximity and familiarity.

01:31:04.020 --> 01:31:06.620
In addition to the afore
mentioned capacity,

01:31:06.620 --> 01:31:08.330
to remain mindful not only of

01:31:08.330 --> 01:31:10.600
a person's current fallen state,

01:31:10.600 --> 01:31:13.220
but also of their potential for goodness.

01:31:13.220 --> 01:31:15.390
Sometimes this intimate adjudication

01:31:15.390 --> 01:31:17.780
takes the form of embodied practice.

01:31:17.780 --> 01:31:21.260
Adeliza judges the Countess best

01:31:21.260 --> 01:31:22.930
when she knows her least.

01:31:22.930 --> 01:31:25.540
Before she knows that she is her mother.

01:31:25.540 --> 01:31:27.650
She can discern her moral capacity

01:31:27.650 --> 01:31:29.170
through the gentleness with which

01:31:29.170 --> 01:31:32.580
the Countess brushes
away a tear on a cheek.

01:31:32.580 --> 01:31:34.560
For her part, the Countess admits

01:31:34.560 --> 01:31:37.280
that before she regularly
and directly interacted

01:31:37.280 --> 01:31:39.690
with the less fortunate of her community,

01:31:39.690 --> 01:31:41.040
she misjudged them,

01:31:41.040 --> 01:31:42.920
viewing them through
the lens of privilege,

01:31:42.920 --> 01:31:45.640
akin to the just world hypothesis.

01:31:45.640 --> 01:31:47.317
as it is now called.

01:31:47.317 --> 01:31:52.129
She admits, "and if a pensive
visage crossed my path,

01:31:52.129 --> 01:31:56.230
I deem the wearer envious or ill natured."

01:31:56.230 --> 01:31:58.310
After her husband's death however,

01:31:58.310 --> 01:32:00.880
she immerses herself in
the community of the poor,

01:32:00.880 --> 01:32:03.913
and ceases to judge others so harshly.

01:32:05.360 --> 01:32:07.450
Benedict acknowledges
that the healing touch

01:32:07.450 --> 01:32:09.240
for which the Countess is known,

01:32:09.240 --> 01:32:11.873
is beyond his own skill set.

01:32:13.160 --> 01:32:14.620
When the Countess refuses to

01:32:15.705 --> 01:32:16.810
divulge her secret to him in Act I,

01:32:16.810 --> 01:32:19.629
he recommends that she
attend another holy man,

01:32:19.629 --> 01:32:23.053
whose sanctity is marked by his gifts.

01:32:24.131 --> 01:32:25.640
Miracles spring from his touch.

01:32:25.640 --> 01:32:27.030
Yet for the Countess,

01:32:27.030 --> 01:32:28.630
judging and redeeming those

01:32:28.630 --> 01:32:31.082
who have made mistakes,

01:32:31.082 --> 01:32:34.070
is not a matter of extreme measures.

01:32:34.070 --> 01:32:37.250
Of lengthy pilgrimages and mere miracles.

01:32:37.250 --> 01:32:40.520
Instead, it involves every
day embodied practice.

01:32:40.520 --> 01:32:43.123
Listening to a tale, wiping away a tear.

01:32:44.090 --> 01:32:46.269
The model of judgment
endorsed by the play,

01:32:46.269 --> 01:32:50.610
encourages refraining from judging those

01:32:50.610 --> 01:32:52.320
with whom one cannot encounter

01:32:52.320 --> 01:32:54.820
in regular familiar proximity.

01:32:54.820 --> 01:32:57.213
Recounting his participation

01:32:57.213 --> 01:32:58.390
in battles with Turkish soldiers

01:32:58.390 --> 01:33:00.080
over territory that would later

01:33:01.001 --> 01:33:03.063
become to be known as Budapest,

01:33:03.063 --> 01:33:04.200
Edmund asks, and this is a quote

01:33:04.200 --> 01:33:06.060
from the quotation from the play.

01:33:06.060 --> 01:33:07.160
"Befits it me,

01:33:07.160 --> 01:33:09.190
Sprung from a race of heroes,

01:33:09.190 --> 01:33:14.190
Narbonne's prince, to lend my
casual arm's approved valor.

01:33:14.330 --> 01:33:18.320
quarrles, nor my countries nor my own.

01:33:18.320 --> 01:33:21.210
To stain my sword of random blood!

01:33:21.210 --> 01:33:23.350
I fought at Buda 'gainst the Turk--

01:33:23.350 --> 01:33:24.773
I smote the turban'd race:

01:33:25.699 --> 01:33:28.590
Did zeal, or did ambition nerve my blow!

01:33:28.590 --> 01:33:31.351
or matter'd it to me, on Buda's domes?

01:33:31.351 --> 01:33:34.729
Whether the crescent or
the cross prevailed."

01:33:34.729 --> 01:33:37.050
Edmund asks whether he should have

01:33:37.050 --> 01:33:39.810
abstained from intervening in quarrels

01:33:39.810 --> 01:33:41.600
to which he is not a party.

01:33:41.600 --> 01:33:44.070
Applied more broadly, he asks whether

01:33:44.070 --> 01:33:46.780
one should judge those
whom one does not know.

01:33:46.780 --> 01:33:48.870
Whose redeeming qualities are not known

01:33:48.870 --> 01:33:50.960
and cannot be invoked to balance

01:33:50.960 --> 01:33:53.300
against their supposed crimes.

01:33:53.300 --> 01:33:55.960
His question recalls
the practice of judgment

01:33:55.960 --> 01:33:58.793
endorsed in "Letter from Xo Ho,

01:33:59.912 --> 01:34:01.250
A Chinese philosopher in London

01:34:02.179 --> 01:34:03.870
to his friend Lien Chi at Peking."

01:34:03.870 --> 01:34:07.003
Which Walpole published in 1757.

01:34:07.003 --> 01:34:10.610
A year before he completed
"The Mysterious Mother."

01:34:10.610 --> 01:34:13.580
In that letter, the Chinese philosopher.

01:34:13.580 --> 01:34:15.680
Of the title disapproves
of the way in which

01:34:15.680 --> 01:34:17.720
the English conduct trials,

01:34:17.720 --> 01:34:19.660
but his apparent disapproval is tempered

01:34:19.660 --> 01:34:22.690
by his explicit demurall from judgment.

01:34:22.690 --> 01:34:24.920
Reason in China, he writes,

01:34:24.920 --> 01:34:26.750
is not reason in England.

01:34:26.750 --> 01:34:28.300
My friend Lien Chi,

01:34:28.300 --> 01:34:30.400
I tell thee things as they are.

01:34:30.400 --> 01:34:32.840
I pretend not to account for the conduct

01:34:32.840 --> 01:34:34.030
of the Englishmen.

01:34:34.030 --> 01:34:37.633
I told thee before they
are incomprehensible.

01:34:38.470 --> 01:34:41.430
Peter Kitson has suggested
that the letter espouses,

01:34:41.430 --> 01:34:43.950
quote, the view that China was or could be

01:34:43.950 --> 01:34:45.964
the locus of moral authority.

01:34:45.964 --> 01:34:48.270
As opposed to a corrupt Britain.

01:34:48.270 --> 01:34:50.030
I see the letter as exemplifying

01:34:50.030 --> 01:34:54.033
a universely available mode of judgment.

01:34:55.130 --> 01:34:57.270
Giving Edmund's explicit aversion

01:34:58.136 --> 01:35:00.290
to imposing his own judgment
on those he does not know,

01:35:00.290 --> 01:35:02.210
the unusual history of Narbonne,

01:35:02.210 --> 01:35:05.610
is an area that had a different point,

01:35:05.610 --> 01:35:08.550
substantial Jewish and Muslim populations

01:35:08.550 --> 01:35:12.130
may shed more light on
Walpole's mysterious tragedy.

01:35:12.130 --> 01:35:14.660
Some earlier critics did
not devote much attention

01:35:14.660 --> 01:35:16.480
to the significance of the area,

01:35:16.480 --> 01:35:18.790
in which the play takes place.

01:35:18.790 --> 01:35:22.663
In her 1974, critical edition
of "The Mysterious Mother,"

01:35:24.370 --> 01:35:25.350
Janet Adele Dolan asserts,

01:35:25.350 --> 01:35:27.370
setting in the mysterious mother

01:35:27.370 --> 01:35:28.760
is relatively unimportant,

01:35:28.760 --> 01:35:30.820
as far as plot is concerned.

01:35:30.820 --> 01:35:33.440
Walpole chose to locate
his play in Narbonne,

01:35:33.440 --> 01:35:35.140
a region in Southern France.

01:35:35.140 --> 01:35:36.700
She leaves it at that.

01:35:36.700 --> 01:35:39.910
Yet, Narbonne has a centuries old history

01:35:39.910 --> 01:35:42.100
as the site of religious toleration,

01:35:42.100 --> 01:35:43.393
and non judgment.

01:35:44.240 --> 01:35:46.370
A place where non Christian persons

01:35:46.370 --> 01:35:49.330
could at various points live in harmony

01:35:49.330 --> 01:35:50.530
and practice their faith,

01:35:50.530 --> 01:35:53.080
without legal restrictions or persecution.

01:35:53.080 --> 01:35:55.970
Without being judged
for what they believed.

01:35:55.970 --> 01:35:57.833
Narbonne is an important
site for Jewish and Muslim

01:35:57.833 --> 01:36:00.600
as well as Christian history.

01:36:00.600 --> 01:36:03.450
Reflecting a critical mass of
scholarship on the subject,

01:36:03.450 --> 01:36:04.570
the scholar Benzion Netantyahu

01:36:05.740 --> 01:36:08.800
contends that in the 700s Narbonne played

01:36:08.800 --> 01:36:10.210
an unprecedented role,

01:36:10.210 --> 01:36:11.860
in the history of European Jewry.

01:36:13.110 --> 01:36:15.630
In The Mysterious Mother,
the Countess argues

01:36:15.630 --> 01:36:18.290
that a person should be
judged by their good works,

01:36:18.290 --> 01:36:19.790
not by their faith.

01:36:19.790 --> 01:36:22.170
Countering Benedict's dictates.

01:36:22.170 --> 01:36:24.310
Similar privileging of works

01:36:24.310 --> 01:36:27.040
as a measure by which
character should be judged,

01:36:27.040 --> 01:36:28.590
facilitated the thriving of

01:36:28.590 --> 01:36:30.720
the Jewish community of Narbonne.

01:36:30.720 --> 01:36:33.150
In the 700's they were
lauded by the kings,

01:36:33.150 --> 01:36:34.660
for sharing their wealth.

01:36:34.660 --> 01:36:36.180
For the public good.

01:36:36.180 --> 01:36:38.540
Recognizing the services
rendered by the Jews

01:36:38.540 --> 01:36:41.167
in relieving the economic
distress of his empire,

01:36:41.167 --> 01:36:43.580
Charles overruled decrees,

01:36:43.580 --> 01:36:47.380
and acted against the Jews
by Christian legislators

01:36:47.380 --> 01:36:50.180
and granted the Jews of
Narbonne basic freedoms,

01:36:50.180 --> 01:36:52.670
and the full protection of his government

01:36:52.670 --> 01:36:55.105
exemption from judgment.

01:36:55.105 --> 01:36:56.650
For the judgment of bigotry.

01:36:56.650 --> 01:36:58.010
Judge by their works,

01:36:58.010 --> 01:36:59.550
rather than by their faith.

01:36:59.550 --> 01:37:01.370
The Jewish inhabitants of Narbonne

01:37:01.370 --> 01:37:03.020
were able to live in harmony,

01:37:03.020 --> 01:37:05.300
with Christians for several decades.

01:37:05.300 --> 01:37:06.620
Indeed at some points,

01:37:06.620 --> 01:37:08.720
non Christians ruled Narbonne,

01:37:08.720 --> 01:37:11.000
for a four decade period in the 700's.

01:37:11.000 --> 01:37:13.690
Islamic forces gained control of the area,

01:37:13.690 --> 01:37:16.890
and used it as a base
for military campaigns.

01:37:16.890 --> 01:37:19.589
Inside a Catholic church,
they built a Mosque.

01:37:19.589 --> 01:37:21.380
Through its setting then,

01:37:21.380 --> 01:37:23.720
the play reiterates Walpole's own call,

01:37:23.720 --> 01:37:25.020
in his other writings,

01:37:25.020 --> 01:37:27.430
for reason to replace bigotry,

01:37:27.430 --> 01:37:29.530
in judging those of different faiths.

01:37:29.530 --> 01:37:33.255
As one he defended the so
called Jew bill of 1753,

01:37:33.255 --> 01:37:35.520
which would have expanded the rights

01:37:35.520 --> 01:37:37.280
of Jewish people living in England,

01:37:37.280 --> 01:37:40.330
but was repealed due
to nativist sentiment.

01:37:40.330 --> 01:37:42.430
There are other examples of authors,

01:37:42.430 --> 01:37:44.330
writing, reiterating,

01:37:44.330 --> 01:37:48.470
the debate over the Jew
bill in the early 1760's.

01:37:48.470 --> 01:37:50.603
To be qualified to
judge others adequately,

01:37:50.603 --> 01:37:52.970
"The Mysterious Mother" suggests,

01:37:52.970 --> 01:37:56.090
one would do well to practice
intimate adjudication.

01:37:56.090 --> 01:37:58.920
To define a person, not
only by a singular crime,

01:37:58.920 --> 01:38:01.790
but also by the balance
of their character.

01:38:01.790 --> 01:38:04.227
To interact frequently
and directly with others,

01:38:04.227 --> 01:38:06.030
whom one judges,

01:38:06.030 --> 01:38:07.750
and to refrain from judging those

01:38:07.750 --> 01:38:09.530
in communities with whom one does

01:38:09.530 --> 01:38:11.880
not enjoy close acquaintance.

01:38:11.880 --> 01:38:14.219
Perhaps the kindest law,

01:38:14.219 --> 01:38:18.448
for would be judges to keep
in mind, the play suggests,

01:38:18.448 --> 01:38:20.772
is to remain cognizant of the extent

01:38:20.772 --> 01:38:24.940
to which they remain un or
at least not yet cognizant.

01:38:24.940 --> 01:38:25.773
Thank you.

01:38:26.692 --> 01:38:29.692
(audience applause)

01:38:33.170 --> 01:38:36.633
- Who would like to get
our discussion started?

01:38:46.420 --> 01:38:48.460
Tasha, there's a mic behind you.

01:38:48.460 --> 01:38:49.293
Thank you.

01:38:50.490 --> 01:38:52.310
- [Tasha] Hello, can you hear me?

01:38:52.310 --> 01:38:53.500
Well thanks so much to all of you

01:38:53.500 --> 01:38:54.900
for your papers which were

01:38:54.900 --> 01:38:57.380
really stimulating and thought provoking.

01:38:57.380 --> 01:38:59.391
Just because it's on my mind,

01:38:59.391 --> 01:39:01.270
I have a question about the last paper.

01:39:01.270 --> 01:39:02.620
I was wondering.

01:39:02.620 --> 01:39:03.600
I guess I was just wondering,

01:39:03.600 --> 01:39:05.388
if you could reflect a little bit

01:39:05.388 --> 01:39:07.678
on the relationship between adjudication

01:39:07.678 --> 01:39:09.560
and the mystery structure,

01:39:09.560 --> 01:39:13.280
which may also sort of
be a point of contact

01:39:13.280 --> 01:39:14.410
with some of the other papers.

01:39:14.410 --> 01:39:17.390
I was thinking about, how the prefix, un,

01:39:17.390 --> 01:39:21.140
has both the two qualities
that you pointed out,

01:39:21.140 --> 01:39:24.230
so that it can cancel something out

01:39:24.230 --> 01:39:26.160
that's already happened.

01:39:26.160 --> 01:39:27.070
Retroactive negation.

01:39:27.070 --> 01:39:29.300
It can be, what was the second one?

01:39:29.300 --> 01:39:33.420
A sort of state of
purity or non existence,

01:39:33.420 --> 01:39:34.740
and it seems that the third

01:39:34.740 --> 01:39:36.700
that might be relevant
for the mystery plot

01:39:36.700 --> 01:39:38.920
is that it can be an anticipatory un.

01:39:38.920 --> 01:39:43.353
So unheard can be something
that isn't yet spoken,

01:39:44.620 --> 01:39:46.040
and I guess I was just wondering,

01:39:46.040 --> 01:39:47.100
it was very interesting to me

01:39:47.100 --> 01:39:49.250
to think about the play as staging

01:39:49.250 --> 01:39:51.430
a scene of judgment,

01:39:51.430 --> 01:39:53.930
or an alternative confession in some way.

01:39:53.930 --> 01:39:57.342
As opposed to the
epistemological question,

01:39:57.342 --> 01:39:59.374
that is at the heart of the mystery.

01:39:59.374 --> 01:40:03.547
This sort of, how did the
maybe more prurient interest

01:40:04.600 --> 01:40:08.030
in what is true,

01:40:08.030 --> 01:40:09.000
how does that intersect,

01:40:09.000 --> 01:40:11.700
with the moral questions
that you're asking

01:40:11.700 --> 01:40:13.140
us to pay attention to?

01:40:13.140 --> 01:40:13.973
- Thank you,

01:40:13.973 --> 01:40:15.960
and I do think that
connotation is certainly.

01:40:15.960 --> 01:40:18.520
That third connotation
certainly is there as well.

01:40:18.520 --> 01:40:21.110
One thing I noticed is
especially in the roughly

01:40:21.110 --> 01:40:22.990
the first half of the play or so,

01:40:22.990 --> 01:40:26.455
is you see this prefix un,

01:40:26.455 --> 01:40:30.314
invoked by the Countess in
ways that tantalizes others.

01:40:30.314 --> 01:40:33.634
Whether consciously or unconsciously.

01:40:33.634 --> 01:40:36.100
To know more about her tale.

01:40:36.100 --> 01:40:37.950
To draw it out of her,

01:40:37.950 --> 01:40:40.300
and I'm also thinking about

01:40:40.300 --> 01:40:41.400
how that connects up,

01:40:41.400 --> 01:40:44.340
with the composition history,

01:40:44.340 --> 01:40:46.883
of The Mysterious Mother
too, that Dale mentioned,

01:40:47.764 --> 01:40:49.902
this alternating disclosure,

01:40:49.902 --> 01:40:53.900
and retrenchment in which
Walpole himself engages.

01:40:53.900 --> 01:40:56.930
It's this notion that we're anticipating

01:40:56.930 --> 01:40:59.183
this disclosure that hasn't yet arrived.

01:41:06.360 --> 01:41:07.193
- Misty.

01:41:10.010 --> 01:41:11.940
- [Misty] Thanks everyone
for those papers.

01:41:11.940 --> 01:41:13.390
I have a question for Matthew,

01:41:13.390 --> 01:41:14.623
but it might fan out.

01:41:15.562 --> 01:41:17.040
I think you've done a beautiful job,

01:41:17.040 --> 01:41:22.040
showing us us that queer
space, of gothic obsession

01:41:22.175 --> 01:41:25.470
in Walpole, and that kind of bifurcation

01:41:25.470 --> 01:41:27.160
which we talked about a little yesterday,

01:41:27.160 --> 01:41:30.630
between the aesthetic of Catholicism.

01:41:30.630 --> 01:41:32.107
An aesthetics that is Catholic,

01:41:32.107 --> 01:41:34.270
and a theology that's Catholic.

01:41:34.270 --> 01:41:35.915
I'm wondering if, and I realize

01:41:35.915 --> 01:41:39.300
you're coming at this as an art historian,

01:41:39.300 --> 01:41:43.873
but is there a kind of
queer theology that

01:41:46.300 --> 01:41:51.300
this crypto Catholicism is articulating

01:41:52.070 --> 01:41:55.810
that's more than just this
same sex erotic space.

01:41:55.810 --> 01:41:59.223
If there's a sort of properly
theological queerness.

01:42:00.500 --> 01:42:02.902
- I think that there definitely is,

01:42:02.902 --> 01:42:05.100
whether Walpole understood
it to be as such,

01:42:05.100 --> 01:42:06.360
I'm less certain.

01:42:06.360 --> 01:42:08.979
There is of course a
growing debate about this,

01:42:08.979 --> 01:42:13.940
in terms of scholarship on camp and performance,

01:42:13.940 --> 01:42:16.127
and the extent to which
these things offer.

01:42:16.127 --> 01:42:18.190
Rather than seeing camp as something,

01:42:18.190 --> 01:42:21.210
that's something kind of facile, and
simply just performative

01:42:21.210 --> 01:42:22.610
in sync with performance.

01:42:22.610 --> 01:42:25.381
Actually camp offers a
really vital and serious way

01:42:25.381 --> 01:42:29.140
of actually engaging with these issues

01:42:29.140 --> 01:42:31.885
outside of any other kind of performative mode.

01:42:31.885 --> 01:42:34.000
I think that may be a really interesting

01:42:34.000 --> 01:42:35.040
way to start into this.

01:42:35.040 --> 01:42:38.707
Because things like these
masses they actually do are,

01:42:38.707 --> 01:42:42.320
at least in terms of Walpole's circle
quite odd and quite strange,

01:42:42.320 --> 01:42:44.230
and I can't think of
many others that are done

01:42:44.230 --> 01:42:45.833
with at least a certain
degree of reverence,

01:42:45.833 --> 01:42:48.000
because in that particular letter,

01:42:48.000 --> 01:42:51.120
Walpole says that they had some masses.

01:42:51.120 --> 01:42:52.250
They pulled down some mass books.

01:42:52.250 --> 01:42:53.940
They had a really good time with incense

01:42:53.940 --> 01:42:55.890
and then they went and played cards.

01:42:55.890 --> 01:42:59.950
There is a kind of in the letter,

01:42:59.950 --> 01:43:01.950
it does actually lead you from something

01:43:02.872 --> 01:43:03.831
which is quite sacred,

01:43:03.831 --> 01:43:05.950
to something which is quite literally fun.

01:43:05.950 --> 01:43:07.570
But actually I don't think
that actually diminishes

01:43:07.570 --> 01:43:09.290
the first part of that,
which is important to me,

01:43:09.290 --> 01:43:11.242
so in my work I try to come back

01:43:11.242 --> 01:43:13.290
with a little bit more gravity.

01:43:13.290 --> 01:43:14.790
If that answers your question.

01:43:15.632 --> 01:43:17.700
- Just to get back to that,

01:43:17.700 --> 01:43:22.270
I think a lot of the work
of 18th century gothic

01:43:22.270 --> 01:43:24.860
is actively to queer Catholicism.

01:43:24.860 --> 01:43:27.670
Both as a kind of institution

01:43:27.670 --> 01:43:29.680
or a historical practice,

01:43:29.680 --> 01:43:31.866
but also as a theology.

01:43:31.866 --> 01:43:36.866
What happens, in something
like Lewis's "The Monk."

01:43:37.900 --> 01:43:40.190
The figure of Mary is queer.

01:43:40.190 --> 01:43:41.810
And there's an erotic relationship

01:43:41.810 --> 01:43:46.810
Ambrosia has with the figure of Mary,

01:43:48.301 --> 01:43:51.400
and that's to exclude
all of those fantasies

01:43:51.400 --> 01:43:54.910
about same sex queer desiring

01:43:54.910 --> 01:43:57.103
in spaces like convents and monasteries.

01:43:58.695 --> 01:43:59.870
Love in a convent.

01:43:59.870 --> 01:44:02.343
Things like that.

01:44:05.463 --> 01:44:07.440
But I think Protestant
writers of gothic

01:44:07.440 --> 01:44:10.943
are certainly involved in the
queering of Catholicism.

01:44:13.780 --> 01:44:16.010
- I think David has
a question and the mic.

01:44:16.010 --> 01:44:17.930
So we'll go next to David.

01:44:19.567 --> 01:44:21.745
- [David] This is for Cheryl.

01:44:21.745 --> 01:44:26.745
Orphaning is a deliberate
act of power really,

01:44:30.760 --> 01:44:32.230
so I'm just trying to think,

01:44:32.230 --> 01:44:33.860
whether you have any other examples,

01:44:33.860 --> 01:44:35.780
or I'm just trying to run through

01:44:35.780 --> 01:44:38.350
all my 18th century plays I know,

01:44:38.350 --> 01:44:41.230
that certainly do often,

01:44:41.230 --> 01:44:43.486
the way that you described it

01:44:43.486 --> 01:44:44.586
is very very powerful.

01:44:46.806 --> 01:44:51.044
Are there any other novels
or plays that you've found,

01:44:51.044 --> 01:44:55.100
where the mother of the parent 
is in control?

01:44:58.295 --> 01:45:00.380
- Usually it's the opposite,

01:45:00.380 --> 01:45:04.900
where there's that
mystery that no one knows

01:45:04.900 --> 01:45:06.750
and it gets revealed at the end,

01:45:06.750 --> 01:45:08.520
and I'm trying to think of

01:45:08.520 --> 01:45:10.070
one that involves a mother

01:45:10.070 --> 01:45:12.650
that is actively orphaning a child,

01:45:12.650 --> 01:45:14.590
and I'm not coming up with
one off the top of my head.

01:45:14.590 --> 01:45:16.400
I don't know if anybody
else can think of any,

01:45:16.400 --> 01:45:18.230
but I'll keep pondering it.

01:45:18.230 --> 01:45:20.710
I think that the structure though,

01:45:20.710 --> 01:45:23.940
of this figure that is clearly,

01:45:23.940 --> 01:45:26.555
has a mystery about their parentage.

01:45:26.555 --> 01:45:30.510
We know that's at the heart
of so many 18th century texts,

01:45:30.510 --> 01:45:34.010
and that does wind up
really working its way

01:45:34.010 --> 01:45:35.080
into the gothic,

01:45:35.080 --> 01:45:37.820
and winds up being a
key way of structuring

01:45:37.820 --> 01:45:39.760
multiple mysteries,

01:45:39.760 --> 01:45:42.130
and I would say concentrated then

01:45:42.130 --> 01:45:44.897
in the orphan itself,

01:45:44.897 --> 01:45:46.268
and I'm just trying to think of,

01:45:46.268 --> 01:45:49.157
lots of the novels of
Charlotte Smith for example,

01:45:49.157 --> 01:45:51.770
focus on an orphan figure,

01:45:51.770 --> 01:45:54.770
that ultimately has to be reclaimed.

01:45:54.770 --> 01:45:56.242
I guess all of the novels of Burbey.

01:45:56.242 --> 01:45:58.778
We could probably start
naming lots of them.

01:45:58.778 --> 01:46:03.680
That sense of a rejected
child, being reclaimed,

01:46:03.680 --> 01:46:07.350
but usually it revolves
around the father figure,

01:46:07.350 --> 01:46:11.883
and not such a purposeful orphaning.

01:46:13.590 --> 01:46:14.810
Yeah, later Bleak House.

01:46:14.810 --> 01:46:17.180
I think Bleak House gives us

01:46:17.180 --> 01:46:20.210
that sort of post 18th century clarity

01:46:20.210 --> 01:46:22.268
of how important orphaning is

01:46:22.268 --> 01:46:24.966
to the structuring of the family.

01:46:24.966 --> 01:46:29.966
Again, bringing in all the
legal aspects of orphaning.

01:46:30.765 --> 01:46:33.170
But I think that's why,
I would say that's why

01:46:33.170 --> 01:46:34.600
maybe this play, I would,

01:46:34.600 --> 01:46:36.880
I think we can probably
continue to think of examples

01:46:36.880 --> 01:46:38.010
but I say that was one of the things that

01:46:38.010 --> 01:46:39.980
makes this play horrific in a way,

01:46:39.980 --> 01:46:43.047
is that it is because of a mother

01:46:43.047 --> 01:46:47.080
purposely choosing to orphan a child.

01:46:47.080 --> 01:46:49.215
If we think of incest as one pole

01:46:49.215 --> 01:46:52.380
of a horrific act a
parent should never do,

01:46:52.380 --> 01:46:56.903
I think the willful
disclaiming of a child,

01:46:56.903 --> 01:46:58.540
and positioning a child as an orphan

01:46:58.540 --> 01:47:00.683
is another sort of horrific act.

01:47:02.580 --> 01:47:04.300
- Yeah, and I'm just going pick up,

01:47:04.300 --> 01:47:09.300
and connect that to something
Nicole talked about, Nicole Garret.

01:47:10.387 --> 01:47:12.830
I was so interested Cheryl,

01:47:12.830 --> 01:47:15.593
that you describe the father's death.

01:47:15.593 --> 01:47:18.340
Even if the mother surviving legally,

01:47:18.340 --> 01:47:21.722
most cases that would
make a child an orphan,

01:47:21.722 --> 01:47:26.160
but as you say, the Countess in a way

01:47:26.160 --> 01:47:27.680
is acting like a father,

01:47:27.680 --> 01:47:30.983
in her act of orphaning her own child.

01:47:30.983 --> 01:47:33.660
It's so much more common in Evelina.

01:47:35.198 --> 01:47:40.198
Evelina has been in effect
disclaimed, by her father.

01:47:40.661 --> 01:47:43.220
So I'm interested in the idea of the Countess

01:47:43.220 --> 01:47:45.859
as a mysterious father,

01:47:45.859 --> 01:47:48.247
but there's some way in which

01:47:48.247 --> 01:47:50.462
she is part of her crime as,

01:47:50.462 --> 01:47:54.030
being more like a father than mother,

01:47:54.030 --> 01:47:56.907
or in those crazy relations,

01:47:56.907 --> 01:48:00.560
where the incest doubles,

01:48:00.560 --> 01:48:05.076
mother mistress mother of
your sister daughter wife,

01:48:05.076 --> 01:48:08.696
that already the Countess has somehow

01:48:08.696 --> 01:48:12.650
put herself in a position
of the dead father,

01:48:12.650 --> 01:48:14.800
and I just wondered if any of you Cheryl

01:48:14.800 --> 01:48:17.030
or Nicole or others, want to?

01:48:17.030 --> 01:48:20.802
- I think it's not very
uncommon in fictions

01:48:20.802 --> 01:48:23.000
that feature single mothers

01:48:23.000 --> 01:48:25.540
for them to take on part of that role,

01:48:25.540 --> 01:48:27.959
or to resist that role
and also for it to be

01:48:27.959 --> 01:48:31.210
regardless of the familial situation

01:48:31.210 --> 01:48:32.043
or the money situation,

01:48:32.043 --> 01:48:34.759
to be seen as unnatural and masculine.

01:48:34.759 --> 01:48:37.783
We see that with "Roxana."

01:48:37.783 --> 01:48:39.100
It's in "Sidney Bidulph."

01:48:39.100 --> 01:48:41.230
I don't know if in "The Natural Daughter"

01:48:41.230 --> 01:48:44.970
you could call that willful orphaning,

01:48:44.970 --> 01:48:48.060
but it was one of the
ones that came to mind,

01:48:48.060 --> 01:48:51.709
but there's usually little
choice in the matter.

01:48:51.709 --> 01:48:56.002
This is going back into the
last question a little bit.

01:48:56.002 --> 01:49:00.061
Thinking about the fatherhood,

01:49:00.061 --> 01:49:02.000
what is intriguing to me,

01:49:02.000 --> 01:49:05.140
is at what point does she become a father,

01:49:05.140 --> 01:49:06.826
and whether we can connect that,

01:49:06.826 --> 01:49:11.460
to the original incest act.

01:49:11.460 --> 01:49:15.362
Does she become a
tyrannical gothic father,

01:49:15.362 --> 01:49:18.490
along the lines of the Count of Narbonne,

01:49:18.490 --> 01:49:20.730
in the Jephson play later,

01:49:20.730 --> 01:49:22.770
at the moment their husband dies?

01:49:22.770 --> 01:49:24.990
Is that why she's capable of that act?

01:49:24.990 --> 01:49:29.990
Is it this situation where she moves from

01:49:30.892 --> 01:49:33.000
the maternal and the female,

01:49:33.000 --> 01:49:36.080
into the paternal and
gothic sort of fatherhood.

01:49:36.080 --> 01:49:37.710
I'm not really sure,

01:49:37.710 --> 01:49:40.317
but in terms of behaving like a father

01:49:40.317 --> 01:49:42.530
that is just something that comes up

01:49:42.530 --> 01:49:45.270
in single mother fictions
of the period a lot,

01:49:45.270 --> 01:49:47.365
and that they really
do try to wrestle with

01:49:47.365 --> 01:49:50.053
in a number of different ways.

01:49:51.309 --> 01:49:53.610
- I think I would build on that and say

01:49:53.610 --> 01:49:55.230
probably she steps into that role

01:49:55.230 --> 01:49:59.001
of the tyrannical father
when she banishes Edmund.

01:49:59.001 --> 01:50:01.830
That sort of decision to remove him,

01:50:01.830 --> 01:50:05.500
which again I would say
is an orphaning-like act,

01:50:05.500 --> 01:50:07.860
but that seems to be where she really

01:50:07.860 --> 01:50:10.390
positions herself as powerful.

01:50:10.390 --> 01:50:12.312
But I think that the play
is very interested in

01:50:12.312 --> 01:50:17.073
thinking through economic
motherhood, fatherhood,

01:50:17.073 --> 01:50:18.830
and the gendering of that,

01:50:18.830 --> 01:50:20.090
but also the emotions.

01:50:20.090 --> 01:50:21.970
I think that's where,

01:50:21.970 --> 01:50:25.360
it's a lot of fun the way

01:50:25.360 --> 01:50:27.760
it was put up on the stage last night,

01:50:27.760 --> 01:50:29.640
to sort of highlight this idea

01:50:29.640 --> 01:50:31.630
of the emotions of the mother.

01:50:31.630 --> 01:50:33.810
At some points she can be imperious,

01:50:33.810 --> 01:50:35.529
and take on a certain masculinity,

01:50:35.529 --> 01:50:38.970
but at other points she seems to be

01:50:38.970 --> 01:50:43.243
very feminine, fainting and shocked.

01:50:44.170 --> 01:50:45.837
- If we think of the play

01:50:45.837 --> 01:50:46.710
as containing vestiges of Lear,

01:50:46.710 --> 01:50:48.390
which I see everywhere in the play.

01:50:48.390 --> 01:50:50.763
There's also echoes of the 
rejection of Cordelia.

01:50:52.651 --> 01:50:57.651
- But I think like the genre
or the mode that he inaugurates,

01:50:58.777 --> 01:51:02.978
Walpole's work is thoroughly preoccupied

01:51:02.978 --> 01:51:06.335
with what we might call
the paternal function.

01:51:06.335 --> 01:51:11.335
Rather than actual biological paternity,

01:51:11.710 --> 01:51:14.640
he has an interest what much later

01:51:14.640 --> 01:51:17.920
psychoanalysts would call
the paternal metaphor,

01:51:17.920 --> 01:51:22.560
or the range of functions
usually wielded by a father

01:51:22.560 --> 01:51:24.540
who is not necessarily
the biological father,

01:51:24.540 --> 01:51:27.050
and I think what Walpole, what happens

01:51:27.050 --> 01:51:29.920
in both Otranto and elsewhere in Walpole,

01:51:29.920 --> 01:51:33.865
is that he's interested in
drawing a very firm distinction

01:51:33.865 --> 01:51:38.865
between an outdated version
of the paternal function--

01:51:39.090 --> 01:51:41.594
a tyrannous perverse father,

01:51:41.594 --> 01:51:44.163
like Manfred in "Otranto,"

01:51:44.163 --> 01:51:48.887
with whom he juxtaposes in Otranto,

01:51:48.887 --> 01:51:51.220
a range of enlightened fathers,

01:51:51.220 --> 01:51:53.090
who are much more sentimentally engaged,

01:51:53.090 --> 01:51:56.320
and who weep when they're
reconciled to their children.

01:51:56.320 --> 01:51:57.590
There's a very firm distinction,

01:51:57.590 --> 01:52:00.057
between types of fathers in "Otranto,"

01:52:00.057 --> 01:52:03.480
but I think that follows through
to "The Mysterious Mother,"

01:52:03.480 --> 01:52:07.520
where you have an outdated
version of the paternal function.

01:52:07.520 --> 01:52:09.770
The kind of tyrannous father.

01:52:09.770 --> 01:52:11.710
The father of perverse enjoyments

01:52:11.710 --> 01:52:13.490
that Freud will talk about

01:52:13.490 --> 01:52:14.817
in "Totem and Taboo."

01:52:15.776 --> 01:52:20.590
In the form of fathers or
friars, Benedict and Martin,

01:52:20.590 --> 01:52:22.542
as I see it with the Catholic past.

01:52:22.542 --> 01:52:25.537
Nonetheless he removed
the paternal function

01:52:25.537 --> 01:52:27.060
from the Count,

01:52:27.060 --> 01:52:31.171
and preempting psychoanalytic
accounts of desire

01:52:31.171 --> 01:52:34.550
and law and paternity, incest erupts,

01:52:34.550 --> 01:52:38.220
when you've moved the paternal function,

01:52:38.220 --> 01:52:39.850
so she has to kind of step into

01:52:39.850 --> 01:52:41.780
the role of a reformed father.

01:52:41.780 --> 01:52:43.910
Reformed in all senses of that word,

01:52:43.910 --> 01:52:46.060
in order to keep the paternal function,

01:52:46.060 --> 01:52:47.290
and with that, the law,

01:52:47.290 --> 01:52:50.460
the circulation of desire, etc. operating.

01:52:50.460 --> 01:52:52.320
I think Walpole is deeply interested

01:52:52.320 --> 01:52:54.600
in the paternal function,

01:52:54.600 --> 01:52:56.350
and drawing distinctions between

01:52:56.350 --> 01:52:59.975
very blandly put, good and bad fathers.

01:52:59.975 --> 01:53:03.806
- She gets to be both types, of father

01:53:03.806 --> 01:53:08.806
the reformed and the outdated.

01:53:09.086 --> 01:53:10.920
And isn't that Walpole
constructed in his own life,

01:53:10.920 --> 01:53:11.780
in multiple fathers.

01:53:11.780 --> 01:53:13.440
Horace Mann, who is one of these.

01:53:13.440 --> 01:53:15.159
There's many many others,

01:53:15.159 --> 01:53:16.650
and in my own work I explore

01:53:16.650 --> 01:53:19.700
both family romance and
queer family romance.

01:53:19.700 --> 01:53:21.100
This possibility of adopting

01:53:22.799 --> 01:53:24.460
an alternate non biological family.

01:53:24.460 --> 01:53:28.900
Could someone say something
about Walpole as an orphan?

01:53:28.900 --> 01:53:30.470
Not just his contested parentage,

01:53:30.470 --> 01:53:32.700
but the idea of being an orphan,

01:53:32.700 --> 01:53:34.187
also as a kind of
interesting queer metaphor

01:53:34.187 --> 01:53:35.407
in and of itself potentially,

01:53:35.407 --> 01:53:37.007
but could someone speak to that?

01:53:38.260 --> 01:53:40.110
- Would you just say a
little bit more about,

01:53:40.110 --> 01:53:41.619
just go down the path you were going,

01:53:41.619 --> 01:53:42.570
that it's a queer metaphor.

01:53:42.570 --> 01:53:44.195
Explain that a little bit more.

01:53:44.195 --> 01:53:45.074
What you're thinking.

01:53:45.074 --> 01:53:46.250
That's great.

01:53:46.250 --> 01:53:48.941
- In particular cultures, the orphan,

01:53:48.941 --> 01:53:51.838
queer child being essentially the orphan,

01:53:51.838 --> 01:53:56.157
often without family
or distance from family

01:53:56.157 --> 01:53:57.063
for that reason,

01:53:58.028 --> 01:53:59.150
and Walpole's own contested parentage

01:53:59.150 --> 01:54:01.720
of course makes him something
potentially of an orphan.

01:54:01.720 --> 01:54:03.384
Distance from his own father too.

01:54:03.384 --> 01:54:08.384
His rather emotional
relationship with his own mother

01:54:09.510 --> 01:54:11.160
which is also somewhat contested.

01:54:13.395 --> 01:54:15.186
- I would take a stab at that by saying,

01:54:15.186 --> 01:54:16.780
that I think the orphan allows there to be

01:54:16.780 --> 01:54:19.420
the act of construction of the family.

01:54:19.420 --> 01:54:21.730
Rather than relying on biological bonds.

01:54:21.730 --> 01:54:23.430
It's sort of erasing that

01:54:23.430 --> 01:54:24.989
as the main structure of the family.

01:54:24.989 --> 01:54:26.963
It opens up all these possibilities

01:54:26.963 --> 01:54:29.980
for what is the structure of
the family going to be then?

01:54:29.980 --> 01:54:31.870
If it's not biological bonds,

01:54:31.870 --> 01:54:33.760
it can be what you want it to be.

01:54:33.760 --> 01:54:35.920
So it opens up that possibility

01:54:35.920 --> 01:54:37.520
for queering I think.

01:54:37.520 --> 01:54:38.780
That's just a quick response,

01:54:38.780 --> 01:54:41.040
but I think that's what again,

01:54:41.040 --> 01:54:42.640
is sort of being played around with,

01:54:42.640 --> 01:54:44.000
with "The Mysterious Mother,"

01:54:44.000 --> 01:54:47.840
if you're actively erasing family bonds,

01:54:47.840 --> 01:54:48.790
that are biological,

01:54:48.790 --> 01:54:51.518
then what do you put in its place?

01:54:51.518 --> 01:54:53.707
I think that could be something

01:54:53.707 --> 01:54:55.805
that's seen as strategic,

01:54:55.805 --> 01:54:57.770
in a way that you're getting at

01:54:57.770 --> 01:54:59.115
that is empowering,

01:54:59.115 --> 01:55:01.410
and I think with "The Mysterious Mother,"

01:55:01.410 --> 01:55:04.780
we see the downside or
the alternative of that,

01:55:04.780 --> 01:55:08.076
where it becomes a ultimately,

01:55:08.076 --> 01:55:10.610
it's a source of power,

01:55:10.610 --> 01:55:13.369
but ultimately, it's gonna
turn back in on itself,

01:55:13.369 --> 01:55:15.373
and become the very source of downfall.

01:55:17.660 --> 01:55:20.070
I guess I'd want to think
about it a bit more.

01:55:20.070 --> 01:55:21.870
I think that what I would say is that

01:55:21.870 --> 01:55:23.360
without biological bonds,

01:55:23.360 --> 01:55:25.120
you're therefore able to create bonds

01:55:25.120 --> 01:55:26.380
that are very text based,

01:55:26.380 --> 01:55:28.256
or like you were emphasizing ritual based.

01:55:28.256 --> 01:55:30.760
It opens up all these other forms

01:55:30.760 --> 01:55:32.650
of constructing relationships,

01:55:32.650 --> 01:55:36.745
and putting the power in
those forms of construction.

01:55:36.745 --> 01:55:37.742
- I must admit,

01:55:37.742 --> 01:55:39.120
I hadn't thought of it
until I heard your paper.

01:55:39.120 --> 01:55:40.485
Thank you very much.

01:55:40.485 --> 01:55:42.053
- Well I'm just thinking of it now.

01:55:42.053 --> 01:55:43.255
- Bouncing off that,

01:55:43.255 --> 01:55:44.088
I'm also thinking about,

01:55:44.088 --> 01:55:45.590
as you say creating textual bonds.

01:55:45.590 --> 01:55:48.120
Walpole's work on the writing of history

01:55:48.120 --> 01:55:50.010
and historiography connected with

01:55:50.010 --> 01:55:54.480
the idea of the queer
entity and no future.

01:55:54.480 --> 01:55:56.550
The writing of history as a substitute

01:55:56.550 --> 01:55:58.533
for biological progeny in a way.

01:55:59.843 --> 01:56:02.200
- But I think this comes across strongly

01:56:02.200 --> 01:56:03.918
in "The Mysterious Mother" in particular.

01:56:03.918 --> 01:56:05.960
If we're going to think about the orphan

01:56:05.960 --> 01:56:07.940
as a queer figure,

01:56:07.940 --> 01:56:10.081
what is crucial is that
the orphan's desire

01:56:10.081 --> 01:56:12.760
is not oedipalized, and
cannot be oedipalized.

01:56:12.760 --> 01:56:15.660
It's deterritorialized, in
deleuzian and guattarian sense,

01:56:16.987 --> 01:56:21.270
but what is interesting
is the threat that poses

01:56:21.270 --> 01:56:22.590
to any kind of symbolic system,

01:56:22.590 --> 01:56:24.670
because look at how Adaliza

01:56:24.670 --> 01:56:27.980
is continuously coerced into the convent

01:56:27.980 --> 01:56:29.060
or into her marriage.

01:56:29.060 --> 01:56:31.579
Her desire has to be managed,

01:56:31.579 --> 01:56:33.870
and oedipalized in a sense,

01:56:33.870 --> 01:56:36.480
because she even though
she's not really an orphan

01:56:36.480 --> 01:56:38.229
but she believes herself to be an orphan.

01:56:38.229 --> 01:56:43.229
Her way of desiring exceeds 
oedipal triangles

01:56:43.684 --> 01:56:48.684
and safely controllable modes of desiring.

01:56:50.010 --> 01:56:51.260
- At the same time,

01:56:51.260 --> 01:56:53.020
one thing that's strange
to me about the play

01:56:53.020 --> 01:56:58.020
is how often Adeliza's
consent is explicitly sought.

01:56:58.350 --> 01:57:00.850
It's of course disavowed or ignored later,

01:57:00.850 --> 01:57:03.120
but there's this explicit request.

01:57:03.120 --> 01:57:04.720
Do you consent?

01:57:04.720 --> 01:57:06.293
At least twice in the play.

01:57:08.320 --> 01:57:10.824
- I think we have a couple more questions,

01:57:10.824 --> 01:57:12.560
and just a tiny bit more time,

01:57:12.560 --> 01:57:14.237
so let's get into.

01:57:14.237 --> 01:57:16.460
- [Georgina] Quite a lot of
what I was going to ask

01:57:16.460 --> 01:57:17.892
has already been covered,

01:57:17.892 --> 01:57:20.740
with the very lovely
discussion about orphaning,

01:57:20.740 --> 01:57:25.372
but I was going to say
something about incest.

01:57:25.372 --> 01:57:27.810
Actually unsay what I said yesterday

01:57:27.810 --> 01:57:29.770
about incest being the
worst thing after murder.

01:57:29.770 --> 01:57:34.770
In fact, the thing that incest
really harms is the child.

01:57:35.050 --> 01:57:37.570
You could get away with having incest

01:57:37.570 --> 01:57:39.739
if both people were infertile.

01:57:39.739 --> 01:57:41.363
You're not gonna have children.

01:57:42.304 --> 01:57:46.110
I also think that in that particular

01:57:46.110 --> 01:57:48.090
familial environment, if
we're gonna call it that.

01:57:48.090 --> 01:57:53.090
There is no respect for
maternal or paternal love.

01:57:53.361 --> 01:57:58.150
What's prioritized all
the time is sexual desire,

01:57:58.150 --> 01:58:01.152
and what we could call love or passion,

01:58:01.152 --> 01:58:05.650
and I do think that she is
a very masculine character.

01:58:05.650 --> 01:58:07.490
One of the things she wants,

01:58:07.490 --> 01:58:09.320
masculine but also feminine.

01:58:09.320 --> 01:58:11.566
She has to behave like a man,

01:58:11.566 --> 01:58:13.560
because she wants the state.

01:58:13.560 --> 01:58:15.180
She wants to keep control of the state,

01:58:15.180 --> 01:58:18.370
which is nobody's really covered the fact,

01:58:18.370 --> 01:58:22.930
that women and territory
is very important,

01:58:22.930 --> 01:58:25.441
and in a way I think that's the way

01:58:25.441 --> 01:58:27.930
that she can hold on to
who she's pretending to be

01:58:27.930 --> 01:58:30.186
as long as she's got that,

01:58:30.186 --> 01:58:32.140
this is mine.

01:58:32.140 --> 01:58:33.030
The other thing I'd like to say,

01:58:33.030 --> 01:58:34.740
is that actually Edmund doesn't seem to

01:58:34.740 --> 01:58:36.480
give a toss about his dad either.

01:58:36.480 --> 01:58:40.280
I mean, nobody's really looked

01:58:40.280 --> 01:58:42.340
at what it's doing to him,

01:58:42.340 --> 01:58:43.980
to live in that environment.

01:58:43.980 --> 01:58:45.680
He ends up being a sort of orphan as well,

01:58:45.680 --> 01:58:48.780
but he's got a narrative as well,

01:58:48.780 --> 01:58:52.540
and I think her journey if you want

01:58:52.540 --> 01:58:55.030
is about discovering
a degree of femininity

01:58:55.030 --> 01:58:58.000
in the way people understand femininity

01:58:58.000 --> 01:59:01.270
as beneath men,

01:59:01.270 --> 01:59:04.530
and one of the things that she
can defend herself from that

01:59:04.530 --> 01:59:07.250
is by keeping control of the state.

01:59:07.250 --> 01:59:09.290
She says the state is mine,

01:59:09.290 --> 01:59:11.900
and I will let you know what to do with.

01:59:11.900 --> 01:59:13.113
Thank you very much.

01:59:13.980 --> 01:59:16.220
I think that that's a very important,

01:59:16.220 --> 01:59:17.550
especially at that time.

01:59:17.550 --> 01:59:19.050
I don't know what Walpole
thought about that,

01:59:19.050 --> 01:59:21.473
but he's certainly looking at it.

01:59:27.960 --> 01:59:30.729
- I think the thing about incest

01:59:30.729 --> 01:59:33.020
that I find intriguing,

01:59:33.020 --> 01:59:36.994
takes me back to Jill's
wonderful article on the play,

01:59:36.994 --> 01:59:41.223
is how heterosexual incest,
in the late 18th century,

01:59:43.078 --> 01:59:46.340
is also a cipher for a
much more unspeakable

01:59:46.340 --> 01:59:49.220
form of desire which
is homo erotic desire.

01:59:49.220 --> 01:59:52.243
It's the most visible of the so-called

01:59:52.243 --> 01:59:54.990
sexual perversions

01:59:54.990 --> 01:59:57.650
which have yet to be named
and constituted as such,

01:59:57.650 --> 02:00:02.650
but occupy the same amorphous category
called sodomy in the period

02:00:03.040 --> 02:00:05.920
and heterosexual incest is often used

02:00:05.920 --> 02:00:08.290
as a cipher for same sex desiring.

02:00:08.290 --> 02:00:11.440
Certainly in Walpole,
but also in Beckford,

02:00:11.440 --> 02:00:12.373
and in Lewis.

02:00:13.748 --> 02:00:16.553
That's what Jill's written on.

02:00:18.618 --> 02:00:22.970
- Walpole often links his love

02:00:22.970 --> 02:00:24.750
for his male correspondents

02:00:24.750 --> 02:00:28.223
and his general coterie of male friends,

02:00:29.085 --> 02:00:31.440
to just by proximity in his letters

02:00:31.440 --> 02:00:35.083
to these often comic
and grotesque anecdotes

02:00:35.083 --> 02:00:38.510
about heterosexual incest.

02:00:38.510 --> 02:00:41.886
Joe has a question, or a thought.

02:00:41.886 --> 02:00:42.719
- [Joe] Thank you.

02:00:42.719 --> 02:00:45.570
Yes, it's thrilling to hear
these papers individually

02:00:45.570 --> 02:00:47.370
and in an ensemble of creating

02:00:47.370 --> 02:00:49.860
a whole context of world,

02:00:49.860 --> 02:00:53.363
in Walpole's mind, in the theater,

02:00:54.576 --> 02:00:58.040
and its various contributory features,

02:00:58.040 --> 02:00:59.390
but before we break,

02:00:59.390 --> 02:01:03.468
I wonder if anyone has a
kind word for Sophocles.

02:01:03.468 --> 02:01:06.468
(audience laughing)

02:01:08.190 --> 02:01:11.003
Exile, return, discovering.

02:01:11.880 --> 02:01:13.193
The mother gets it.

02:01:15.230 --> 02:01:17.070
Right on the case.

02:01:17.070 --> 02:01:17.903
Early on.

02:01:17.903 --> 02:01:22.203
Maybe even before the
Corinthian messenger arrives.

02:01:23.824 --> 02:01:28.824
- The difference is of
course the level of intent

02:01:29.490 --> 02:01:30.790
and consciousness with which

02:01:30.790 --> 02:01:34.374
the mother certainly commits
the first act of incest.

02:01:34.374 --> 02:01:38.803
Where there is no unconscious inoperation.

02:01:39.740 --> 02:01:42.377
Although Electra and Phaedrus,

02:01:42.377 --> 02:01:45.760
and other plays in
the classical tradition,

02:01:45.760 --> 02:01:48.310
certainly work their way into

02:01:48.310 --> 02:01:51.463
the construction of incest in the play.

02:01:52.400 --> 02:01:54.400
I think Walpole's a bit nervous

02:01:54.400 --> 02:01:56.570
or a bit anxious to engage too closely

02:01:56.570 --> 02:01:58.642
with the classical tradition here,

02:01:58.642 --> 02:02:03.642
because of the broader
political imperatives,

02:02:04.030 --> 02:02:09.030
of resisting French
Neo-classicism in the play.

02:02:09.620 --> 02:02:13.610
He instead would look, as
we discussed last night,

02:02:13.610 --> 02:02:15.970
to other models of tragedy.

02:02:15.970 --> 02:02:18.996
Without necessarily reiterating,

02:02:18.996 --> 02:02:21.743
or following closely,

02:02:22.640 --> 02:02:24.970
a classical tragic formulas,

02:02:24.970 --> 02:02:27.110
but there certainly are records there,

02:02:27.110 --> 02:02:31.250
and this I suppose amplified

02:02:31.250 --> 02:02:33.366
by the fact that,

02:02:33.366 --> 02:02:37.010
once Walpole has written the play,

02:02:37.010 --> 02:02:39.150
several of his friends and correspondents

02:02:39.150 --> 02:02:42.340
write him saying, oh I found

02:02:42.340 --> 02:02:44.850
a version of the story in this text.

02:02:44.850 --> 02:02:46.850
I found a version of story in that text.

02:02:48.605 --> 02:02:51.105
and several people are
almost without undermining

02:02:51.105 --> 02:02:52.020
his claims to original genius.

02:02:52.020 --> 02:02:54.230
Did you know that this happens here?

02:02:54.230 --> 02:02:56.090
He claims with a lot of them,

02:02:56.090 --> 02:02:57.910
not to have known about them,

02:02:57.910 --> 02:03:01.990
but that's only kind of provides grist

02:03:01.990 --> 02:03:04.993
to his own mill that he has
successfully articulated

02:03:04.993 --> 02:03:07.800
something archetypal,

02:03:07.800 --> 02:03:11.850
and something that is in a
kind of cultural unconscious

02:03:11.850 --> 02:03:12.683
and always has been,

02:03:12.683 --> 02:03:14.140
and perhaps always will be,

02:03:14.140 --> 02:03:16.923
and in that way, the play itself,

02:03:17.820 --> 02:03:21.938
aspires to a mythological
status of its own,

02:03:21.938 --> 02:03:24.797
in the way that "Oedipus" has.

02:03:25.974 --> 02:03:26.863
- Maybe you can even think of it,

02:03:27.726 --> 02:03:28.730
as Walpole orphaning himself.

02:03:28.730 --> 02:03:31.170
Severing himself from this classical

02:03:31.170 --> 02:03:32.500
literary genealogy,

02:03:32.500 --> 02:03:35.800
because remember he writes
back and forth to Mason,

02:03:35.800 --> 02:03:40.230
who keeps encouraging him to
revise the Countess character

02:03:40.230 --> 02:03:41.360
so that she's deceived.

02:03:41.360 --> 02:03:44.863
So she doesn't know that she's
encountering her own son.

02:03:45.964 --> 02:03:48.470
There seems to be some
kind of disassociation,

02:03:48.470 --> 02:03:50.483
of that literary lineage.

02:03:52.760 --> 02:03:55.690
- I would say just our
familiarity with Sophocles,

02:03:55.690 --> 02:03:57.666
and I think all of us
know that we're reading

02:03:57.666 --> 02:04:01.640
an incest plot as soon
as we pick up the play,

02:04:01.640 --> 02:04:03.760
but when I was watching last night,

02:04:03.760 --> 02:04:05.370
it was making me think I was

02:04:05.370 --> 02:04:08.250
trying to imagine an original reader,

02:04:08.250 --> 02:04:11.540
which in some ways is the
theme of this session,

02:04:11.540 --> 02:04:12.960
and trying to imagine if

02:04:12.960 --> 02:04:15.200
they would not have known,

02:04:15.200 --> 02:04:18.920
the answer to the mystery was incest.

02:04:18.920 --> 02:04:21.544
If they were figuring it out,

02:04:21.544 --> 02:04:23.100
much earlier in the play,

02:04:23.100 --> 02:04:25.050
or knew it before they
even picked up the play,

02:04:25.050 --> 02:04:26.500
or if they would have been

02:04:26.500 --> 02:04:29.010
shocked at that moment of horror,

02:04:29.010 --> 02:04:31.570
when all is revealed towards the end.

02:04:31.570 --> 02:04:33.944
I was trying to think
through some of the papers

02:04:33.944 --> 02:04:35.267
as they were being given.

02:04:35.267 --> 02:04:37.285
How much the readership would have known

02:04:37.285 --> 02:04:41.460
the scandalous nature of the story.

02:04:41.460 --> 02:04:44.100
Before they've even picked
up the play is probably true,

02:04:44.100 --> 02:04:46.400
but I was trying to figure out,

02:04:46.400 --> 02:04:49.500
the original reader
slash viewer of the play.

02:04:49.500 --> 02:04:52.653
Would they have known the mystery or not?

02:04:57.240 --> 02:04:59.552
- I think we have time just
for this last question.

02:04:59.552 --> 02:05:02.444
- [Man] Thank you so much.

02:05:02.444 --> 02:05:05.470
I have a question that
is specifically for Dale,

02:05:05.470 --> 02:05:09.400
because your paper got me thinking

02:05:09.400 --> 02:05:12.150
about the epigraph on the title page

02:05:12.150 --> 02:05:15.133
of the 1768 from Virgil,

02:05:15.133 --> 02:05:17.754
which seems to just completely embody

02:05:17.754 --> 02:05:21.161
the ambivalence of
revealing and concealing.

02:05:21.161 --> 02:05:22.830
I translated it.

02:05:22.830 --> 02:05:26.150
May it be lawful for me to
speak all that I have heard,

02:05:26.150 --> 02:05:28.411
and this is when the Sybil
is speaking in book six,

02:05:28.411 --> 02:05:30.316
and I also think it's interesting too

02:05:30.316 --> 02:05:34.250
because the Latin, this word fas,

02:05:34.250 --> 02:05:36.030
is actually indeclinable,

02:05:36.030 --> 02:05:39.300
so it has a type of
imbalance, in and of itself,

02:05:39.300 --> 02:05:41.183
and I was just wondering,

02:05:41.183 --> 02:05:42.520
if you considered that at all,

02:05:42.520 --> 02:05:44.240
when you're thinking
about your paper, firstly,

02:05:44.240 --> 02:05:47.131
and secondly, what happens
to that epigraph over time,

02:05:47.131 --> 02:05:50.458
because I think I saw it disappear,

02:05:50.458 --> 02:05:53.730
after the 1768 printing.

02:05:53.730 --> 02:05:55.550
- Can you read me the translation again?

02:05:55.550 --> 02:05:56.383
What is it?

02:05:56.383 --> 02:05:57.320
- [Man] This is my translation.

02:05:57.320 --> 02:05:59.580
May it be lawful for me to speak

02:05:59.580 --> 02:06:00.930
all that I have heard?

02:06:00.930 --> 02:06:01.763
- Okay.

02:06:01.763 --> 02:06:03.520
- [Man] And that word fas is,

02:06:03.520 --> 02:06:06.310
it means divine law,

02:06:06.310 --> 02:06:08.750
or law or rule,

02:06:08.750 --> 02:06:10.104
and it's indeclinable,

02:06:10.104 --> 02:06:13.010
so it's sitting on the line.

02:06:13.010 --> 02:06:14.020
- Okay.

02:06:14.020 --> 02:06:16.502
What is interesting is,

02:06:16.502 --> 02:06:19.700
I haven't thought about the
epigraph in that context

02:06:19.700 --> 02:06:21.580
but what is interesting,

02:06:21.580 --> 02:06:24.180
is it brings to mind his mangling

02:06:24.180 --> 02:06:26.571
of classical sources as epigraph,

02:06:26.571 --> 02:06:31.190
in "Otranto" and he shifts an epigraph,

02:06:31.190 --> 02:06:36.190
and misquotes it from a Latin source,

02:06:36.200 --> 02:06:40.100
but the other thing that strikes me

02:06:40.100 --> 02:06:44.700
is that that seems to
be a classicist version

02:06:44.700 --> 02:06:47.560
of the same sentiments
that would be expressed

02:06:47.560 --> 02:06:50.240
by the ghost of Hamlet's father,

02:06:50.240 --> 02:06:53.553
in Hamlet, I could a tale unfold,

02:06:54.672 --> 02:06:56.702
in the kind of I could a tale unfold.

02:06:56.702 --> 02:06:58.228
That would harrow out their soul,

02:06:58.228 --> 02:06:59.610
and freeze thy blood and all the rest,

02:06:59.610 --> 02:07:02.980
which becomes the kind of mantra

02:07:02.980 --> 02:07:05.761
of gothic writing after Walpole.

02:07:05.761 --> 02:07:10.761
So that sense of the ineffable.

02:07:12.380 --> 02:07:14.110
Read it again.

02:07:14.110 --> 02:07:14.960
The translation.

02:07:14.960 --> 02:07:17.010
- [Man] May it be lawful for me to speak

02:07:17.010 --> 02:07:19.723
all that I have heard, and the Latin is
sit mihi fas audita loqui

02:07:21.197 --> 02:07:26.197
- Yeah, the ineffable.

02:07:28.567 --> 02:07:30.138
Things that come to mind are

02:07:30.138 --> 02:07:32.710
notions of the ineffable,

02:07:32.710 --> 02:07:35.670
about the kind of subjunctive mood

02:07:35.670 --> 02:07:40.670
that we get in I could
a tale unfold in Hamlet.

02:07:41.340 --> 02:07:43.480
A kind of reserve of horror

02:07:43.480 --> 02:07:47.860
that is not quite articulable descriptive.

02:07:47.860 --> 02:07:50.530
That which lies beyond
the powers of language,

02:07:50.530 --> 02:07:52.080
and indeed representation

02:07:52.080 --> 02:07:54.960
and I think there is a concern

02:07:54.960 --> 02:07:56.313
in The Mysterious Mother,

02:07:57.150 --> 02:07:59.030
of the unrepresentable,

02:07:59.030 --> 02:08:02.373
and that which can only
be circled by language.

02:08:02.373 --> 02:08:04.623
There's a lot of stuff
that happens off stage

02:08:04.623 --> 02:08:06.100
that we don't see,

02:08:06.100 --> 02:08:09.026
and a lot of stuff that
can't be represented.

02:08:09.026 --> 02:08:13.070
Which later becomes this concern

02:08:13.070 --> 02:08:16.174
with the ineffable in gothic writing.

02:08:16.174 --> 02:08:19.690
That's kind of how I would approach that,

02:08:19.690 --> 02:08:23.377
although I haven't thought
about the epigraph.

02:08:23.377 --> 02:08:24.490
- Perspectively it also looks

02:08:24.490 --> 02:08:26.880
toward a period of greater freedom

02:08:26.880 --> 02:08:28.000
in a certain sense doesn't it,

02:08:28.000 --> 02:08:29.980
that I will be able to do these things.

02:08:29.980 --> 02:08:31.620
It actually really arguably,

02:08:31.620 --> 02:08:33.520
is actually a kind of Latin paraphrase,

02:08:33.520 --> 02:08:37.080
or another version of fari quar sentiat
say what one feels,

02:08:37.080 --> 02:08:39.090
which of course is Walpole's own motto.

02:08:39.090 --> 02:08:40.460
So potentially it is actually

02:08:40.460 --> 02:08:42.560
another version of that basic idea

02:08:42.560 --> 02:08:45.013
about liberty in a sense.

02:08:49.940 --> 02:08:51.740
- I cut a little bit out of the prologue

02:08:51.740 --> 02:08:56.000
that I recited at the beginning

02:08:56.000 --> 02:08:57.100
of the performance yesterday,

02:08:57.100 --> 02:08:59.300
which was actually about how

02:08:59.300 --> 02:09:01.480
ridiculing the French,

02:09:01.480 --> 02:09:03.260
who were so rule bound,

02:09:03.260 --> 02:09:06.231
for having anything really significant

02:09:06.231 --> 02:09:08.940
or horrific take place off stage,

02:09:08.940 --> 02:09:10.890
and here in England we
don't have to do that.

02:09:10.890 --> 02:09:13.360
It doesn't have to be
reported by a gazette.

02:09:13.360 --> 02:09:15.380
You're going to see it,

02:09:15.380 --> 02:09:20.380
but easier to just ascribe
this problem to the French

02:09:21.164 --> 02:09:26.003
than actually in fact, Walpole,

02:09:28.431 --> 02:09:29.353
this whole wonderful set of papers,

02:09:30.210 --> 02:09:31.723
makes me think he doesn't want

02:09:31.723 --> 02:09:32.556
to simply put it before us.

02:09:32.556 --> 02:09:34.010
He wants to create a space,

02:09:34.010 --> 02:09:36.960
where as you each brought
out in different ways,

02:09:36.960 --> 02:09:38.590
is said and not said,

02:09:38.590 --> 02:09:41.117
or unsaid, or taken back.

02:09:41.117 --> 02:09:45.050
Where things are moving
in and out of the space

02:09:45.050 --> 02:09:47.484
and some kind of graspability

02:09:47.484 --> 02:09:52.180
knowing part is the bed trick

02:09:52.180 --> 02:09:55.305
that a countess knew more than others knew

02:09:55.305 --> 02:09:59.397
and that's part of the
act of transgression.

02:09:59.397 --> 02:10:02.330
Thank you all so much for coming today

02:10:02.330 --> 02:10:04.470
to help us continue to contemplate

02:10:04.470 --> 02:10:06.900
this remarkable and mysterious

02:10:06.900 --> 02:10:07.970
and very rich work,

02:10:07.970 --> 02:10:09.690
and please join me in thanking

02:10:09.690 --> 02:10:11.980
all of our speakers for this

02:10:11.980 --> 02:10:14.870
wonderfully rich and suggestive ensemble

02:10:14.870 --> 02:10:17.518
as Joe put it, of papers.

02:10:17.518 --> 02:10:20.601
(audience applause)

